QZ.load("chem", {"chapters":{"1":["1","States of matter"],"2":["2","Atomic structure"],"3":["3","Chemical bonding"],"4":["4","Chemical formulae and equations"],"5":["5","Chemical calculations"],"6":["6","Electrochemistry"],"7":["7","Chemical energetics"],"8":["8","Rates of reaction"],"9":["9","Reversible reactions and equilibrium"],"10":["10","Redox reactions"],"11":["11","Acids and bases"],"12":["12","Preparation of salts"],"13":["13","The Periodic Table"],"14":["14","Metallic elements and alloys"],"15":["15","Reactivity of metals"],"16":["16","Extraction and corrosion of metals"],"17":["17","Chemistry of our environment"],"18":["18","Introduction to organic chemistry"],"19":["19","Reactions of organic compounds"],"20":["20","Petrochemicals and polymers"],"21":["21","Experimental design and separation techniques"],"22":["22","Chemical analysis"]},"topics":{"chem-form":["Formulae & calculations","Write the formula"],"chem-react":["Reactions & equations","Give the reactions and equations"],"chem-law":["Laws, theories & rules","State it"],"chem-expl":["Describe & explain","Describe or explain"],"chem-diff":["Differences","State the differences"],"chem-test":["Tests (notes)","Describe the test and result"],"chem-prop":["Properties","Recall and explain"],"chem-uses":["Uses","Give the uses and the property behind each"],"chem-facts":["Key points","Answer"]},"cards":[["n-c1-exp-changes-of-state","chem-expl",1,"Changes of state","<p><strong>Melting</strong> (solid → liquid), <strong>boiling</strong> (liquid → gas at b.p.), <strong>evaporating</strong> (liquid → gas at the surface below b.p.), <strong>freezing</strong> (liquid → solid), <strong>condensing</strong> (gas → liquid). Melting, boiling and evaporating <strong>take in</strong> energy; freezing and condensing <strong>give out</strong> energy.</p>",0,""],["n-c1-law-kinetic-particle-theory-of","chem-law",1,"Kinetic particle theory of matter","<ol>\n<li>All matter is made of tiny <strong>particles</strong> (atoms, molecules or ions). 2. The particles are <strong>always moving</strong>. 3. The <strong>higher the temperature, the faster</strong> they move (more kinetic energy). 4. There are <strong>forces of attraction</strong> between particles. 5. Heavier particles move more slowly than lighter ones at the same temperature.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Explaining changes of state</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: on <strong>heating</strong>, particles gain kinetic energy; in a solid they <strong>vibrate more</strong> until they overcome the forces holding them in fixed positions → <strong>melting</strong>. In a liquid, particles move faster until they have enough energy to <strong>overcome the attractive forces</strong> and escape as gas → <strong>boiling</strong>. On <strong>cooling</strong> the reverse happens: particles lose energy, forces pull them together → <strong>condensing</strong>, <strong>freezing</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c1-dif-solids-liquids-and-gases","chem-diff",1,"Solids, liquids and gases","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Solid</th><th>Liquid</th><th>Gas</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td><strong>Fixed</strong> shape</td><td><strong>No fixed shape</strong> — takes the shape of the container</td><td><strong>No fixed shape</strong> — fills the container</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume</td><td><strong>Fixed</strong></td><td><strong>Fixed</strong></td><td><strong>Not fixed</strong> — fills whole container</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compressible?</td><td>No</td><td>Hardly</td><td><strong>Yes, easily</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flow?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Particle <strong>separation</strong></td><td>Very <strong>close together</strong> (touching)</td><td><strong>Close together</strong> (mostly touching)</td><td><strong>Far apart</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Particle <strong>arrangement</strong></td><td><strong>Regular</strong>, fixed pattern (lattice)</td><td><strong>Irregular</strong> / random</td><td><strong>Random</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Particle <strong>motion</strong></td><td><strong>Vibrate</strong> about fixed positions</td><td><strong>Move around / slide past</strong> each other</td><td>Move <strong>fast</strong> and <strong>randomly</strong> in all directions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Forces between particles</td><td>Strong</td><td>Weaker</td><td>Very weak (negligible)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c1-dif-evaporation-vs-boiling","chem-diff",1,"Evaporation vs boiling","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Evaporation</th><th>Boiling</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Only at the <strong>surface</strong></td><td><strong>Throughout</strong> the liquid (bubbles form)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature</td><td><strong>Any</strong> temperature below b.p.</td><td>Only <strong>at</strong> the boiling point</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slow</td><td>Fast</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c1-pro-heating-and-cooling-curves","chem-prop",1,"Heating and cooling curves","<p>On a <strong>heating curve</strong> temperature rises, then stays <strong>constant (flat)</strong> at the melting point and again at the boiling point. The flat parts are where the <strong>state changes</strong>: energy taken in is used to <strong>overcome the forces between particles</strong>, not to raise the temperature (kinetic energy). On a <strong>cooling curve</strong> the flat parts are at the boiling/condensing point and the melting/freezing point: energy is <strong>given out</strong> as the forces form, so temperature stays constant. A <strong>pure</strong> substance gives a <strong>sharp</strong> flat section; an impure one gives a sloping section over a range.</p>",1,""],["n-c1-pro-effect-of-temperature-and","chem-prop",1,"Effect of temperature and pressure on the volume of a gas","<p><strong>Increasing temperature</strong> (constant pressure) → volume <strong>increases</strong>: particles gain kinetic energy, move faster, hit the walls harder and more often, so push them outwards. <strong>Increasing pressure</strong> (constant temperature) → volume <strong>decreases</strong>: particles are pushed closer together (gases are easily compressed because the particles are far apart).</p>",1,""],["n-c1-pro-effect-of-relative-molecul","chem-prop",1,"Effect of relative molecular mass on diffusion","<p><strong>The lower the relative molecular mass (Mr), the faster a gas diffuses</strong> — lighter particles move faster at the same temperature. e.g. <strong>ammonia (Mr 17) and hydrogen chloride (Mr 36.5)</strong> in a long tube: the white ring of <strong>ammonium chloride</strong> forms <strong>nearer the HCl end</strong>, because NH₃ diffuses faster. Diffusion is also faster at <strong>higher temperature</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c1-fac-pure-substances-have-a-sha","chem-facts",1,"How do melting and boiling points show purity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pure substances have a sharp m.p. and b.p.; mixtures melt and boil over a range.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Used to test purity.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c1-fac-state-symbols-s-solid-l-li","chem-facts",1,"The four state symbols?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">State symbols: (s) solid, (l) liquid, (g) gas, (aq) dissolved in water.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-proton-number","chem-form",2,"Proton number","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Z = number of protons</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Also equals the number of electrons in a neutral atom (atoms have no overall charge).</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-mass-number","chem-form",2,"Mass number","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">A = number of protons + number of neutrons</div>",0,""],["n-c2-for-electrons-in-an-atom","chem-form",2,"Electrons in an atom","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">number of electrons = Z</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">For an atom only. For an ion: electrons = Z − charge. e.g. Na⁺: 11 − 1 = 10 electrons; Cl⁻: 17 + 1 = 18 electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-number-of-neutrons","chem-form",2,"Number of neutrons","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">neutrons = A − Z</div>",0,""],["n-c2-for-the-atomic-mass-standard","chem-form",2,"The atomic mass standard","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">1 atomic mass unit = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">12</span></span> × mass of one atom of carbon-12</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">This is why Ar is defined \"compared with 1/12 of the mass of an atom of ¹²C\".</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-relative-atomic-mass-from","chem-form",2,"Relative atomic mass from isotopes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Ar = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">(mass₁ × % abundance₁) + (mass₂ × % abundance₂)</span><span class=\"de\">100</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. chlorine: 75% ³⁵Cl and 25% ³⁷Cl → Ar = (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) ÷ 100 = 35.5.</p>",1,""],["n-c2-for-atomic-symbols","chem-form",2,"Atomic symbols","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>A</span><span>Z</span></span>X</span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Mass number A on top, proton number Z at the bottom. e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>12</span><span>6</span></span>C</span> has 6 p, 6 e, 6 n; <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>35</span><span>17</span></span>Cl</span>⁻ has 17 p, 18 e, 18 n.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-law-electrons-in-shells-bohr-s","chem-law",2,"Electrons in shells (Bohr's model)","<p>What you need is the <strong>shell model</strong>, not the history: <strong>electrons are arranged in shells (energy levels) around the nucleus</strong>, filling the innermost shell first (2, 8, 8…). <strong>Group VIII noble gases have a full outer shell</strong>; <strong>the number of outer-shell electrons equals the group number in Groups I to VII</strong>; <strong>the number of occupied shells equals the period number</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-dif-elements-compounds-and-mix","chem-diff",2,"Elements, compounds and mixtures","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Element</th><th>Compound</th><th>Mixture</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made of</td><td>One type of atom (same proton number)</td><td>Two or more elements <strong>chemically combined</strong></td><td>Two or more substances <strong>not chemically combined</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Composition</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Fixed</strong> proportions (fixed formula)</td><td><strong>Variable</strong> proportions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Properties</td><td>Its own</td><td><strong>Different</strong> from its elements</td><td>Keeps the properties of each component</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Separated by</td><td>Cannot be broken down by chemical methods</td><td>Only by <strong>chemical reactions</strong></td><td><strong>Physical methods</strong> (filtration, distillation, chromatography…)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Fe, O₂, S</td><td>FeS, H₂O, NaCl</td><td>air, sea water, iron + sulfur powder, alloys</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c2-pro-proton-neutron-and-electro","chem-prop",2,"Proton, neutron and electron","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Particle</th><th>Relative charge</th><th>Relative mass</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Proton</strong></td><td><strong>+1</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Neutron</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Electron</strong></td><td><strong>−1</strong></td><td><strong>1/1840</strong> (negligible)</td><td>Shells around the nucleus</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c2-fac-almost-all-the-mass-of-an","chem-facts",2,"Where is almost all the mass of an atom?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Almost all the mass of an atom is in the nucleus.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-fac-atoms-are-neutral-number-o","chem-facts",2,"Why are atoms neutral?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Atoms are neutral: number of protons = number of electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-c3-dif-diamond-vs-graphite","chem-diff",3,"Diamond vs graphite","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Diamond</th><th>Graphite</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bonds per carbon atom</td><td><strong>4</strong> strong covalent bonds</td><td><strong>3</strong> strong covalent bonds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Structure</td><td>Giant 3-D <strong>tetrahedral</strong> network</td><td><strong>Layers</strong> of hexagonal rings; <strong>weak forces between layers</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hardness</td><td><strong>Very hard</strong></td><td><strong>Soft, slippery</strong> — layers slide over each other</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electrical conductivity</td><td><strong>Does not conduct</strong> (no free electrons)</td><td><strong>Conducts</strong> — one <strong>delocalised electron</strong> per carbon atom moves along the layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Melting point</td><td>Very high</td><td>Very high</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uses</td><td><strong>Cutting tools</strong> (drill tips) — very hard</td><td><strong>Lubricant</strong> (layers slide) and <strong>electrodes</strong> (conducts, high m.p., inert)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c3-dif-carbon-dioxide-vs-silicon","chem-diff",3,"Carbon dioxide vs silicon(IV) oxide","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</th><th>Silicon(IV) oxide, SiO₂</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Structure</td><td><strong>Simple molecules</strong> (O=C=O)</td><td><strong>Giant covalent</strong> — each Si bonded to 4 O, each O to 2 Si</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Forces broken on melting/boiling</td><td><strong>Weak intermolecular forces</strong></td><td><strong>Strong covalent bonds</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>State at r.t.p.</td><td>Gas</td><td>Hard solid (sand, quartz)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Melting point</td><td>Very low (sublimes at −78 °C)</td><td>Very high (≈ 1700 °C)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c3-dif-ionic-vs-simple-molecular","chem-diff",3,"Ionic vs simple molecular vs giant covalent vs metallic","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Property</th><th>Ionic</th><th>Simple molecular</th><th>Giant covalent</th><th>Metallic</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Particles</td><td>Ions</td><td>Molecules</td><td>Atoms</td><td>Positive ions + delocalised electrons</td></tr>\n<tr><td>m.p./b.p.</td><td>High</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>Very high</td><td>Usually high</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conducts?</td><td><strong>Only when molten or aqueous</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No (except graphite)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (solid and liquid)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>NaCl, MgO</td><td>H₂O, CH₄, CO₂</td><td>diamond, SiO₂</td><td>Cu, Fe</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c3-pro-simple-molecular-simple-co","chem-prop",3,"Simple molecular (simple covalent) compounds","<p>(a) <strong>Low melting and boiling points</strong> — <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> only <strong>weak intermolecular forces</strong> need to be overcome (the strong covalent bonds inside the molecules are <strong>not</strong> broken). (b) <strong>Poor electrical conductivity</strong> — molecules are <strong>uncharged</strong>; no ions or free electrons. (Specific types of intermolecular force are not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-c3-pro-ionic-compounds","chem-prop",3,"Ionic compounds","<p>(a) <strong>High melting and boiling points</strong> — <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> a <strong>lot of energy</strong> is needed to overcome the <strong>strong electrostatic attraction</strong> between oppositely charged ions in the <strong>giant lattice</strong>. (b) <strong>Good electrical conductivity when molten or aqueous, poor when solid</strong> — <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> when molten/dissolved the <strong>ions are free to move</strong> and carry charge; in the solid the ions are held in fixed positions. Usually soluble in water.</p>",1,""],["n-c3-pro-metals","chem-prop",3,"Metals","<p>(a) <strong>Good electrical conductivity</strong> — the <strong>delocalised electrons can move</strong> through the structure and carry charge. (b) <strong>Malleable</strong> (hammered into shape) <strong>and ductile</strong> (drawn into wires) — the <strong>layers of positive ions can slide over each other</strong> without breaking the metallic bonding (the sea of electrons holds them together in the new position).</p>",1,""],["n-c3-pro-similarity-between-diamond","chem-prop",3,"Similarity between diamond and silicon(IV) oxide","<p>Both are <strong>giant covalent</strong> structures in which every atom is joined to others by <strong>strong covalent bonds</strong> in a 3-D tetrahedral network, so both are <strong>very hard</strong>, have <strong>very high melting points</strong> and <strong>do not conduct electricity</strong> (no free electrons or ions).</p>",1,""],["n-c3-fac-group-i-ions-are-1-group-i","chem-facts",3,"Charges of the ions in Groups I, II, VI and VII?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Group I ions are 1+, Group II 2+, Group VI 2−, Group VII 1−.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Charge = electrons lost (metals) or gained (non-metals).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c3-fac-ionic-compounds-conduct-wh","chem-facts",3,"When do ionic compounds conduct electricity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ionic compounds conduct when molten or dissolved, never as solids.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c4-exp-cross-over-method-formula","chem-expl",4,"Cross-over method (formula from charges)","<p>A quick way to <strong>deduce the formula of an ionic compound from the charges on its ions</strong>: write the ions, <strong>swap the charge numbers</strong> and write them as subscripts, then <strong>simplify</strong>. e.g. Al³⁺ and O²⁻ → Al₂O₃; Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻ → CaCl₂; Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ → Mg₂O₂ → <strong>MgO</strong>. Use <strong>brackets</strong> for compound ions: Ca²⁺ + OH⁻ → <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong>; NH₄⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → <strong>(NH₄)₂SO₄</strong>. Check: total positive charge = total negative charge.</p>",1,""],["n-c4-exp-word-and-symbol-equations","chem-expl",4,"Word and symbol equations","<p>A <strong>word equation</strong> names reactants → products. A <strong>symbol equation</strong> uses formulae and must be <strong>balanced</strong> (same number of each atom on both sides) — change only the <strong>big numbers in front</strong>, never the formulae. Include <strong>state symbols</strong> (s), (l), (g), (aq). e.g. Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> You may be asked to <strong>deduce</strong> an equation from information in the question: work out each formula from the names/ion charges first, then balance.</p>",1,""],["n-c4-law-charges-of-common-ions","chem-law",4,"Charges of common ions","<p>Corrected — see the coloured notes.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Charge</th><th>Positive ions (cations)</th><th>Negative ions (anions)</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>H⁺, Na⁺, K⁺, Li⁺, Ag⁺, <strong>NH₄⁺</strong> (ammonium)</td><td>Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻, <strong>OH⁻</strong> (hydroxide), <strong>NO₃⁻</strong> (nitrate), HCO₃⁻</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Ba²⁺, Zn²⁺, Fe²⁺ (iron(II)), Cu²⁺ (copper(II))</td><td><strong>O²⁻</strong> (oxide), <strong>S²⁻</strong> (sulfide), SO₄²⁻ (sulfate), SO₃²⁻ (sulfite), CO₃²⁻ (carbonate)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Al³⁺, Fe³⁺ (iron(III)), Cr³⁺</td><td>N³⁻ (nitride), PO₄³⁻ (phosphate)</td><td>The oxide ion is <strong>O²⁻</strong>, not O₂²⁻, and sulfide is <strong>S²⁻</strong>, not S₂²⁻ (O₂²⁻ would be a peroxide ion). The Roman numeral in a name gives the charge: iron(III) = Fe³⁺.</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c4-law-relative-masses-compared-w","chem-law",4,"Relative masses compared with carbon-12","<p>Atomic masses are measured <strong>relative to ¹²C = 12 exactly</strong>: on this scale H = 1, He = 4, C = 12, O = 16. (See Ar, item 23.)</p>",0,""],["n-c4-for-reacting-masses-no-moles","chem-form",4,"Reacting masses (no moles)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass of product</span><span class=\"de\">mass of reactant</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Mr of product × its number in the equation</span><span class=\"de\">Mr of reactant × its number in the equation</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Simple proportion. e.g. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂: 100 g CaCO₃ gives 56 g CaO, so 25 g gives 14 g. (Mole calculations: Chapter 5.)</p>",0,""],["n-c4-fac-deducing-a-formula-from-a","chem-facts",4,"How do you deduce a formula from a diagram of a structure?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Deducing a formula from a diagram of a structure</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Count the atoms/ions of each type in the model and write the <strong>simplest ratio</strong> (e.g. a lattice with 4 Ca²⁺ and 8 Cl⁻ → CaCl₂).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c4-fac-diatomic-elements-h-n-o-f","chem-facts",4,"Name the diatomic elements.","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Diatomic elements: H₂, N₂, O₂, F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Always write them with the 2 in equations.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c4-fac-formulae-you-must-know-nam","chem-facts",4,"Formulae you must know (named in the syllabus)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Formulae you must know (named in the syllabus)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>H₂O, CO₂, CO, SO₂, SO₃, NO, NO₂, NH₃, CH₄, HCl, HNO₃, H₂SO₄, NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂, CaO, CaCO₃, NaCl, Fe₂O₃, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, CuSO₄, CoCl₂, AgNO₃, Ba(NO₃)₂, C₂H₅OH, CH₃COOH, C₆H₁₂O₆.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c5-for-amount-of-substance","chem-form",5,"Amount of substance","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass (g)</span><span class=\"de\">molar mass (g/mol)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>mass = moles × molar mass</b> · <b>molar mass = mass ÷ moles</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Molar mass = Ar for an element, Mr for a compound.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-number-of-particles","chem-form",5,"Number of particles","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">number of particles = moles × 6.02 × 10²³</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">6.02 × 10²³ is the Avogadro constant.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-percentage-yield","chem-form",5,"Percentage yield","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% yield = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">actual yield</span><span class=\"de\">theoretical yield</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">The theoretical (predicted) yield is calculated from the equation, using the limiting reactant.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-percentage-purity","chem-form",5,"Percentage purity","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% purity = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass of pure substance</span><span class=\"de\">mass of impure sample</span></span> × 100</div>",1,""],["n-c5-for-volume-of-a-gas-at-r-t-p","chem-form",5,"Volume of a gas at r.t.p.","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">volume (dm³) = moles × 24</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>moles = volume (dm³) ÷ 24</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">With cm³ use 24 000. r.t.p. = room temperature and pressure.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-moles-in-a-solution","chem-form",5,"Moles in a solution","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³)</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>concentration = moles ÷ volume</b> · <b>volume = moles ÷ concentration</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Volume in dm³ = volume in cm³ ÷ 1000.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-concentration-in-g-dm","chem-form",5,"Concentration in g/dm³","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">concentration (g/dm³) = concentration (mol/dm³) × molar mass</div>",1,""],["n-c5-for-reacting-masses-with-moles","chem-form",5,"Reacting masses with moles","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles of A → mole ratio → moles of B</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Step 1: moles of the known substance = mass ÷ Mr. Step 2: use the mole ratio from the balanced equation. Step 3: convert moles of the unknown into mass (× Mr), gas volume (× 24 dm³) or concentration (÷ volume in dm³).</p><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ with 25 g CaCO₃: 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25 mol → 1 : 1 → 0.25 × 56 = 14 g CaO; volume of CO₂ = 0.25 × 24 = 6 dm³.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-empirical-formula","chem-form",5,"Empirical formula","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ratio of atoms = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass (or %) of each element</span><span class=\"de\">Ar</span></span>, then ÷ the smallest</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Then give the simplest whole-number ratio. e.g. 2.4 g Mg + 1.6 g O: 2.4 ÷ 24 = 0.1 and 1.6 ÷ 16 = 0.1 → 1 : 1 → MgO.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-molecular-formula","chem-form",5,"Molecular formula","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">n = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Mr</span><span class=\"de\">empirical formula mass</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Molecular formula = (empirical formula) × n.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-titration-calculation","chem-form",5,"Titration calculation","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = c × V  →  mole ratio  →  c = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">moles</span><span class=\"de\">volume (dm³)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. 25.0 cm³ NaOH neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ HCl: moles HCl = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 → 1 : 1 → c(NaOH) = 0.00200 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-water-of-crystallisation","chem-form",5,"Water of crystallisation","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">x = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">moles of water</span><span class=\"de\">moles of anhydrous salt</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Heat the hydrated salt to constant mass. Moles of anhydrous salt = mass ÷ Mr; moles of water = mass lost ÷ 18. e.g. CuSO₄·xH₂O gives x = 5.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-law-avogadro-constant-6-02-10","chem-law",5,"Value of the Avogadro constant?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ per mole<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c5-law-avogadro-s-law","chem-law",5,"Avogadro's law","<p><strong>Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules</strong> — so one mole of <strong>any</strong> gas has the same volume (the molar gas volume).</p>",0,""],["n-c5-law-r-t-p-room-temperature-and","chem-law",5,"What is r.t.p.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">r.t.p. = room temperature and pressure</p><p><strong>25 °C (298 K) and 101.3 kPa (1 atmosphere).</strong></p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c5-law-molar-gas-volume-24-dm-at","chem-law",5,"Molar gas volume at r.t.p.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Molar gas volume = 24 dm³ at r.t.p.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c5-fac-the-three-mole-equations","chem-facts",5,"The three mole equations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The three mole equations<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>mass = moles × M</strong>; <strong>moles = concentration × volume (dm³)</strong>; <strong>gas volume (dm³) = moles × 24</strong>. Rearrange the one you need.</p></div>",1,""],["n-c5-fac-always-convert-cm-to-dm-10","chem-facts",5,"What must you do to a volume in cm³ before using c × V?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Always convert cm³ to dm³ (÷ 1000) before using c × V.</p>",0,""],["n-c5-fac-round-only-the-final-answe","chem-facts",5,"When do you round in a calculation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Round only the final answer; keep at least 3 s.f. in working.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Give the answer to the s.f. of the least precise data (usually 3 s.f.).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c6-pro-water-in-aqueous-electroly","chem-prop",6,"Which ions does water provide in aqueous electrolytes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Water in aqueous electrolytes provides H⁺ and OH⁻ ions<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p>What matters is that an <strong>aqueous</strong> solution contains <strong>H⁺(aq) and OH⁻(aq) from water</strong> as well as the ions of the dissolved compound — so hydrogen or oxygen may be produced instead of the metal or non-metal. (\"Water is a weak electrolyte\" is not needed.)</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c6-pro-products-at-the-electrodes","chem-prop",6,"Products at the electrodes","<p><strong>Metals or hydrogen form at the cathode; non-metals (other than hydrogen) form at the anode</strong>.<br />\n<strong>Molten binary compound:</strong> metal at cathode, non-metal at anode. e.g. molten <strong>lead(II) bromide</strong> → <strong>lead</strong> (silvery liquid) at cathode, <strong>bromine</strong> (red-brown vapour) at anode.<br />\n<strong>Aqueous solutions</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: <strong>Cathode</strong> — the <strong>less reactive</strong> of the metal and hydrogen is discharged: metals <strong>above hydrogen</strong> in the reactivity series (Na, K, Ca, Mg, Al…) → <strong>hydrogen</strong>; metals below hydrogen (Cu, Ag) → the <strong>metal</strong>. <strong>Anode</strong> — <strong>concentrated halide</strong> → <strong>halogen</strong> (chlorine, bromine, iodine); <strong>dilute halide or no halide</strong> (sulfate, nitrate) → <strong>oxygen</strong>.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Electrolyte (inert electrodes)</th><th>Cathode (−)</th><th>Anode (+)</th><th>Observations / what is left</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Molten lead(II) bromide</td><td>Lead</td><td>Bromine</td><td>Silvery bead of lead; brown fumes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Concentrated</strong> aqueous sodium chloride</td><td><strong>Hydrogen</strong></td><td><strong>Chlorine</strong></td><td>Bubbles at both; solution becomes <strong>sodium hydroxide</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Dilute</strong> sulfuric acid</td><td>Hydrogen</td><td>Oxygen</td><td>Bubbles; volume H₂ : O₂ = <strong>2 : 1</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aqueous copper(II) sulfate (graphite) <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Copper</strong> (pink-brown deposit)</td><td><strong>Oxygen</strong></td><td><strong>Blue colour fades</strong>; solution becomes acidic (sulfuric acid)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aqueous copper(II) sulfate (<strong>copper electrodes</strong>) <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td>Copper deposited (cathode gains mass)</td><td><strong>Copper anode dissolves</strong> (loses mass)</td><td><strong>Blue colour stays the same</strong> (Cu²⁺ removed = Cu²⁺ added)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Dilute</strong> aqueous sodium chloride <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td>Hydrogen</td><td><strong>Oxygen</strong> (+ some chlorine)</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c6-pro-electroplating-set-up","chem-prop",6,"Electroplating set-up","<p><strong>Cathode = the object to be plated</strong>; <strong>anode = the plating metal</strong> (e.g. silver); <strong>electrolyte = aqueous solution of a salt of the plating metal</strong> (e.g. silver nitrate). Anode: Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻ (dissolves); cathode: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag (deposits on the object).</p>",0,""],["n-c6-pro-transfer-of-charge-during","chem-prop",6,"Transfer of charge during electrolysis","<p>(a) <strong>Electrons move through the external circuit</strong> (wires) from the anode to the cathode. (b) At the electrodes <strong>electrons are gained (cathode) or lost (anode)</strong> by ions. (c) In the electrolyte, <strong>ions move</strong>: <strong>cations to the cathode, anions to the anode</strong>. Electrons never travel through the electrolyte.</p>",1,""],["n-c6-pro-half-equations-you-should","chem-prop",6,"Half-equations you should know","<p>Cathode: <strong>Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb</strong>; <strong>2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂</strong>; <strong>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</strong>; <strong>Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al</strong>. Anode: <strong>2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻</strong>; <strong>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</strong>; <strong>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻</strong>; <strong>Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻</strong> (copper anode); <strong>2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻</strong> (aluminium extraction).</p>",1,""],["n-c6-dif-conduction-in-metals-vs-el","chem-diff",6,"Conduction in metals vs electrolytes","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Metallic conduction</th><th>Electrolytic conduction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Particles that carry charge</td><td><strong>Delocalised electrons</strong></td><td><strong>Ions</strong> (moving in opposite directions)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Substance</td><td>Metals, graphite (solid or liquid)</td><td>Molten or aqueous ionic compounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chemical change?</td><td><strong>No</strong> — substance unchanged</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — decomposed; new substances at the electrodes</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c6-dif-hydrogen-oxygen-fuel-cells","chem-diff",6,"Hydrogen–oxygen fuel cells vs petrol engines in vehicles","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Fuel cell</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td><strong>Only product is water</strong> — no CO₂ (greenhouse gas), no CO, no oxides of nitrogen, no particulates; more <strong>efficient</strong> (more energy converted to useful energy); quiet; hydrogen can be made from renewable sources (electrolysis using wind/solar)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td><strong>Hydrogen is a flammable gas — difficult and dangerous to store</strong> (needs high pressure or very low temperature); large, heavy tanks; <strong>few hydrogen filling stations</strong>; fuel cells are <strong>expensive</strong> (platinum catalyst); hydrogen is often made from <strong>fossil fuels</strong> (methane) or using electricity from fossil fuels, which releases CO₂</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c6-fac-electrolysis-of-an-ionic-c","chem-facts",6,"What state must an ionic compound be in for electrolysis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Electrolysis of an ionic compound needs it to be molten or dissolved — solid ionic compounds don't conduct.</p>",0,""],["n-c6-fac-reduction-always-happens-a","chem-facts",6,"At which electrode do reduction and oxidation happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Reduction always happens at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c7-for-enthalpy-change-from-bond","chem-form",7,"Enthalpy change from bond energies","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ΔH = energy taken in breaking bonds − energy given out making bonds</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Negative ΔH = exothermic; positive ΔH = endothermic.</p><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (H–H 436, Cl–Cl 242, H–Cl 431 kJ/mol): broken 436 + 242 = 678; made 2 × 431 = 862 → ΔH = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ/mol (exothermic).</p>",1,""],["n-c7-dif-physical-change-vs-chemica","chem-diff",7,"Physical change vs chemical change","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Physical change</th><th>Chemical change</th></tr>\n<tr><td>New substance formed?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reversible?</td><td>Usually <strong>easily reversed</strong></td><td>Usually <strong>difficult to reverse</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy change</td><td>Usually small</td><td>Often large (heat/light given out or taken in)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mass</td><td>Same substance, same mass</td><td>Total mass conserved but substances change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Melting, boiling, dissolving, filtration, distillation</td><td>Combustion, rusting, neutralisation, electrolysis, cracking</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c7-dif-exothermic-vs-endothermic","chem-diff",7,"Exothermic vs endothermic","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Exothermic</th><th>Endothermic</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Thermal energy</td><td><strong>Given out</strong> to surroundings</td><td><strong>Taken in</strong> from surroundings</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature of surroundings</td><td><strong>Rises</strong></td><td><strong>Falls</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>ΔH <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Negative</strong></td><td><strong>Positive</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Products' energy</td><td><strong>Lower</strong> than reactants</td><td><strong>Higher</strong> than reactants</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bonds <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td>More energy released making bonds than used breaking them</td><td>More energy used breaking bonds than released making them</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c7-pro-reaction-pathway-diagrams","chem-prop",7,"Reaction pathway diagrams (energy level diagrams)","<p>Plot <strong>energy</strong> (y-axis) against <strong>progress of reaction</strong> (x-axis). <strong>Exothermic:</strong> products drawn <strong>lower</strong> than reactants; ΔH arrow points <strong>down</strong>. <strong>Endothermic:</strong> products <strong>higher</strong>; ΔH arrow points <strong>up</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Label <strong>reactants, products, ΔH</strong> (from reactant level to product level) and <strong>Ea</strong> (from reactant level up to the <strong>top of the hump</strong>). Arrows must have heads.</p>",1,""],["n-c7-fac-a-temperature-rise-means-e","chem-facts",7,"Temperature rise or fall: exothermic or endothermic?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A temperature rise means exothermic; a fall means endothermic.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Measure with a thermometer in a polystyrene cup.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-concentrati","chem-law",8,"Effect of concentration on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the concentration of a solution increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> More particles <strong>per unit volume</strong> → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong> → more successful collisions per second.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-temperature","chem-law",8,"Effect of temperature on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the temperature increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Particles have <strong>more kinetic energy</strong> → move faster → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong> AND a <strong>greater proportion of collisions have energy ≥ Ea</strong> (more successful collisions).</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-pressure-of","chem-law",8,"Effect of gas pressure on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the pressure of gases increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Gas particles are <strong>closer together</strong> (more particles per unit volume) → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-surface-are","chem-law",8,"Effect of surface area on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the surface area of a solid increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p>Use <strong>smaller pieces / powder</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> More particles are <strong>exposed</strong> on the surface → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-adding-a-catalyst-increase","chem-law",8,"Effect of a catalyst on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Adding a catalyst increases the rate; removing it decreases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The catalyst <strong>lowers the activation energy</strong>, so a <strong>greater proportion of collisions are successful</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-pro-measuring-the-rate-practic","chem-prop",8,"Measuring the rate — practical methods","<ol>\n<li><strong>Volume of gas produced</strong> — collect in a <strong>gas syringe</strong> (or an upside-down measuring cylinder/burette over water) and read the volume every 10–30 s; e.g. marble chips (CaCO₃) + HCl → CO₂; Mg + HCl → H₂. 2. <strong>Change in mass</strong> — flask on a <strong>balance</strong> with a cotton-wool plug; mass <strong>decreases</strong> as gas escapes. 3. <strong>Time for a precipitate to hide a cross</strong> (\"disappearing cross\": sodium thiosulfate + HCl).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Evaluation:</strong> gas syringe — accurate, but gas may leak / plunger may stick; collection over water — not suitable for gases that dissolve (CO₂ slightly soluble); mass loss — not suitable for gases with low Mr like <strong>hydrogen</strong> (mass change too small); judging \"disappearing cross\" is subjective. Control: same volume and concentration, same mass and size of solid, same temperature.</p>",1,""],["n-c8-pro-interpreting-rate-graphs","chem-prop",8,"Interpreting rate graphs","<p><strong>Steepest gradient = fastest rate.</strong> The curve is <strong>steepest at the start</strong> (highest concentration of reactants) and becomes <strong>flat when the reaction stops</strong> (a reactant used up). <strong>Faster reaction</strong> → steeper curve, finishes sooner, but <strong>same final volume</strong> if the same amounts of reactants are used. <strong>More reactant</strong> (the limiting one) → <strong>more product</strong> (higher final line). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Rate at a point = <strong>gradient of a tangent</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c8-pro-enzymes","chem-prop",8,"Enzymes","<p><strong>Enzymes are proteins that act as biological catalysts</strong> — they increase the rate of reactions in living things (and in fermentation). They work best at an <strong>optimum temperature</strong> (about 37 °C) and pH; at high temperatures they are <strong>denatured</strong> and stop working.</p>",0,""],["n-c8-fac-a-catalyst-increases-the-r","chem-facts",8,"How does a catalyst increase the rate?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A catalyst increases the rate by lowering the activation energy<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>More precisely: a catalyst provides an <strong>alternative pathway with a lower activation energy</strong>, so a greater proportion of collisions have enough energy to react. It is <strong>chemically unchanged</strong> at the end.</p></div>",1,""],["n-c8-fac-a-reaction-is-fastest-at-t","chem-facts",8,"When is a reaction fastest, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A reaction is fastest at the start</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Concentration of reactants is highest, so collisions are most frequent; the rate falls as reactants are used up.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c9-exp-compromise-conditions-why","chem-expl",9,"Compromise conditions (why 450 °C and these pressures)","<p><strong>Temperature:</strong> the forward reaction is <strong>exothermic</strong>, so a <strong>lower temperature gives a higher yield</strong> — but the rate would be <strong>too slow</strong>. 450 °C is a <strong>compromise</strong> giving a reasonable yield at a fast enough rate. <strong>Pressure (Haber):</strong> fewer gas molecules on the right (4 → 2), so <strong>high pressure moves equilibrium to the right (more NH₃) and increases rate</strong> — but very high pressures need <strong>expensive, strong equipment</strong> and are <strong>dangerous</strong> (risk of explosion), so 200 atm is a compromise. <strong>Pressure (Contact):</strong> equilibrium already lies far to the right (yield &gt; 95%) at 2 atm, so higher pressure is <strong>not worth the extra cost</strong>. <strong>Catalyst:</strong> increases the <strong>rate</strong> (equilibrium reached sooner) but does <strong>not</strong> change the yield.</p>",1,""],["n-c9-law-le-chatelier-s-principle-p","chem-law",9,"Le Chatelier's principle — predicting the position of equilibrium","<p><strong>If a change is made to a system at equilibrium, the position of equilibrium moves to oppose (reduce) the change.</strong></p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on position of equilibrium</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase temperature</strong></td><td>Moves in the <strong>endothermic</strong> direction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Decrease temperature</strong></td><td>Moves in the <strong>exothermic</strong> direction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase pressure</strong> (gases)</td><td>Moves to the side with <strong>fewer gas molecules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Decrease pressure</strong></td><td>Moves to the side with <strong>more gas molecules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase concentration</strong> of a reactant</td><td>Moves to the <strong>right</strong> (more products)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Remove a product</strong></td><td>Moves to the <strong>right</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Add a catalyst</strong></td><td><strong>No change</strong> in position — equilibrium is reached <strong>faster</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Use the information in the question (which direction is exothermic, number of gas moles).</p>",1,""],["n-c9-dif-closed-system-vs-open-syst","chem-diff",9,"Closed system vs open system","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Closed system</th><th>Open system</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Substances</td><td><strong>Nothing can enter or leave</strong></td><td>Substances (e.g. gases) can escape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equilibrium?</td><td><strong>Can reach equilibrium</strong></td><td>Cannot reach equilibrium — products escape, reaction goes to completion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Sealed flask / gas syringe</td><td>CaCO₃ heated in an open tube (CO₂ escapes)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c9-dif-single-nutrient-fertiliser","chem-diff",9,"Single-nutrient fertiliser vs NPK (compound) fertiliser","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Single-nutrient fertiliser</th><th>NPK (compound) fertiliser</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Elements supplied</td><td>One essential element, e.g. <strong>nitrogen</strong></td><td><strong>Nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td><strong>Ammonium nitrate</strong>, ammonium sulfate, urea</td><td>Mixture such as <strong>ammonium phosphate + potassium chloride</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>Correct one deficiency</td><td><strong>Improved plant growth</strong> — N for proteins/leaf growth, P for roots, K for flowers/fruit and resistance to disease</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c9-tes-chemical-tests-for-water","chem-test",9,"Chemical tests for water","<p><strong>Anhydrous copper(II) sulfate turns from white to blue.</strong> <strong>Anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride turns from blue to pink</strong> (cobalt chloride paper). These show that <strong>water is present</strong> — not that it is pure.</p>",0,""],["n-c9-tes-test-for-the-purity-of-wat","chem-test",9,"Test for the purity of water","<p><strong>Pure water melts at exactly 0 °C and boils at exactly 100 °C</strong> (at atmospheric pressure). Impurities raise the boiling point and lower the melting point.</p>",0,""],["n-c9-pro-examples-of-reversible-rea","chem-prop",9,"Examples of reversible reactions","<p><strong>Hydrated compounds and heat</strong> (syllabus examples): <strong>CuSO₄·5H₂O(s) ⇌ CuSO₄(s) + 5H₂O(l)</strong> — heating the <strong>blue</strong> hydrated crystals gives <strong>white</strong> anhydrous powder; <strong>adding water</strong> turns it <strong>blue</strong> again (and gets hot). <strong>CoCl₂·6H₂O ⇌ CoCl₂ + 6H₂O</strong> — <strong>pink</strong> hydrated → <strong>blue</strong> anhydrous on heating; water turns it pink again. Industrial examples: Haber and Contact processes. (Also: ammonium chloride ⇌ ammonia + hydrogen chloride on heating.)</p>",0,""],["n-c9-pro-factors-affecting-the-posi","chem-prop",9,"Factors affecting the position of equilibrium","<p><strong>Temperature, pressure (for gases) and concentration</strong> change the position of equilibrium (see law 16). A <strong>catalyst does not</strong> — it only makes equilibrium reached faster.</p>",1,""],["n-c9-pro-fertilisers","chem-prop",9,"Fertilisers","<p><strong>Ammonium salts and nitrates are used as fertilisers</strong> (e.g. ammonium nitrate NH₄NO₃, ammonium sulfate (NH₄)₂SO₄, potassium nitrate). <strong>NPK fertilisers provide nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium for improved plant growth.</strong> Ammonia (Haber) and sulfuric acid (Contact) are used to make them.</p>",0,""],["n-c9-fac-equilibrium-rates-are-equa","chem-facts",9,"At equilibrium: what about the rates and the concentrations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Equilibrium: rates are equal, concentrations are constant (not equal).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Common mistake.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c9-fac-haber-450-c-200-atm-iron-c","chem-facts",9,"Conditions for the Haber and Contact processes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haber: 450 °C, 200 atm, iron. Contact: 450 °C, 2 atm, V₂O₅.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Learn both sets.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c10-tes-tests-for-reducing-and-oxi","chem-test",10,"Tests for reducing and oxidising agents","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Reagent</th><th>What it detects</th><th>Colour change if positive</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong> (an <strong>oxidising</strong> agent)</td><td>A <strong>reducing agent</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td><td>MnO₄⁻ is <strong>reduced</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aqueous potassium iodide</strong> (a <strong>reducing</strong> agent)</td><td>An <strong>oxidising agent</strong></td><td><strong>Colourless → brown</strong> (iodine formed)</td><td>I⁻ is <strong>oxidised</strong> to I₂</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(A drop of starch turns the iodine <strong>blue-black</strong> — not required, but confirms iodine.)</p>",1,""],["n-c10-pro-reducing-agents","chem-prop",10,"Reducing agents","<p><strong>Hydrogen (H₂)</strong>, <strong>carbon (C)</strong>, <strong>carbon monoxide (CO)</strong> — remove oxygen from metal oxides; also <strong>reactive metals</strong> (e.g. Mg, Zn) and <strong>iodide ions (I⁻)</strong>. e.g. CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O (H₂ is oxidised, CuO is reduced); Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂.</p>",1,""],["n-c10-pro-oxidising-agents","chem-prop",10,"Oxidising agents","<p><strong>Oxygen (O₂)</strong>, <strong>hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂)</strong>, <strong>acidified potassium manganate(VII) (KMnO₄)</strong>, <strong>acidified potassium dichromate(VI) (K₂Cr₂O₇)</strong>, <strong>chlorine (Cl₂)</strong>. The dichromate formula is <strong>K₂Cr₂O₇</strong>, not \"KCr₂\".</p>",1,""],["n-c10-pro-rules-for-oxidation-number","chem-prop",10,"Rules for oxidation numbers","<p>(a) <strong>The oxidation number of an element in its uncombined state is zero</strong> (Fe, O₂, Cl₂ = 0). (b) <strong>The oxidation number of a monatomic ion is the same as the charge on the ion</strong> (Na⁺ = +1, O²⁻ = −2). (c) <strong>The sum of the oxidation numbers in a compound is zero.</strong> (d) <strong>The sum of the oxidation numbers in an ion is equal to the charge on the ion.</strong> Usually O = −2, H = +1. (Oxidation number and Roman numerals: item 95, Chapter 13.)<br />\ne.g. Mn in MnO₄⁻: x + 4(−2) = −1 → <strong>x = +7</strong>. S in SO₄²⁻: x − 8 = −2 → <strong>+6</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c10-pro-identifying-redox-reaction","chem-prop",10,"Identifying redox reactions","<p>Look for gain/loss of <strong>oxygen</strong> (Core), gain/loss of <strong>electrons</strong> in ionic equations <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>, or <strong>changes in oxidation number</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>. e.g. <strong>Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu</strong>: Zn loses 2 e⁻ (0 → +2, <strong>oxidised</strong>, the reducing agent); Cu²⁺ gains 2 e⁻ (+2 → 0, <strong>reduced</strong>, the oxidising agent). <strong>Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂</strong>: Cl₂ reduced, I⁻ oxidised.</p>",1,""],["n-c10-fac-every-displacement-reactio","chem-facts",10,"What type of reaction is every displacement reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Every displacement reaction of metals and of halogens is a redox reaction.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c10-fac-combustion-rusting-and-res","chem-facts",10,"Combustion, rusting and respiration are which type of reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Combustion, rusting and respiration are oxidation reactions.</p>",0,""],["n-c11-rea-reactions-of-acids","chem-react",11,"Reactions of acids","<p>(a) <strong>acid + metal → salt + hydrogen</strong>: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (fizzing; gas pops with a lighted splint). (b) <strong>acid + base → salt + water</strong>: CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O; NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. (c) <strong>acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide</strong> (see 77).</p>",0,""],["n-c11-rea-reactions-of-bases","chem-react",11,"Reactions of bases","<p>(a) <strong>base + acid → salt + water</strong> (neutralisation). (b) <strong>base + ammonium salt → salt + water + ammonia</strong>: e.g. 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃ (warm; ammonia turns damp red litmus blue).</p>",0,""],["n-c11-tes-ammonia-turns-damp-red-lit","chem-test",11,"Ammonia turns damp red litmus paper blue","<p>Used to show a base (alkali) has reacted with an ammonium salt — ammonia is the only common <strong>alkaline gas</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c11-pro-indicators","chem-prop",11,"Indicators","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Indicator</th><th>In acid</th><th>In alkali</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Litmus</strong></td><td><strong>Red</strong></td><td><strong>Blue</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Thymolphthalein</strong></td><td><strong>Colourless</strong></td><td><strong>Blue</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Methyl orange</strong></td><td><strong>Red</strong></td><td><strong>Yellow</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Universal indicator</strong> shows the pH: <strong>red (pH 0–2) → orange/yellow (3–6) → green (7, neutral) → blue (8–11) → purple (12–14)</strong>. Lower pH = more acidic = <strong>higher H⁺ concentration</strong>; higher pH = more alkaline. (Phenolphthalein is not in the syllabus.)</p>",0,""],["n-c11-pro-comparing-strong-and-weak","chem-prop",11,"Comparing strong and weak acids","<p>At the <strong>same concentration</strong>, a strong acid has a <strong>lower pH</strong>, conducts electricity <strong>better</strong> and reacts <strong>faster</strong> with metals and carbonates (more H⁺ ions) than a weak acid.</p>",1,""],["n-c11-pro-oxides-and-the-periodic-ta","chem-prop",11,"Oxides and the Periodic Table","<p><strong>Metal oxides are basic</strong>, <strong>non-metal oxides are acidic</strong> — so oxides change from basic to acidic <strong>across a period</strong> (left to right), matching the change from metallic to non-metallic character.</p>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-all-alkalis-are-bases-but","chem-facts",11,"Are all bases alkalis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">All alkalis are bases, but not all bases are alkalis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Alkalis are the <strong>soluble</strong> bases. e.g. copper(II) oxide is a base but not an alkali (insoluble).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-common-alkali-formulae","chem-facts",11,"Formulae of the common alkalis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Common alkali formulae</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sodium hydroxide (caustic soda) <strong>NaOH</strong>; potassium hydroxide (caustic potash) <strong>KOH</strong>; limewater = aqueous calcium hydroxide <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong> (not \"CaOH\" — calcium is 2+, so two OH⁻); aqueous ammonia <strong>NH₃(aq)</strong> (sometimes written NH₄OH, never \"NH₃OH\").</p></div>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-burning-elements-in-oxygen","chem-facts",11,"Burning elements in oxygen: which oxides form?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Burning elements in oxygen — and the oxides formed</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Non-metals: <strong>sulfur</strong> burns with a <strong>blue</strong> flame → SO₂ (acidic); <strong>carbon</strong> glows <strong>red</strong> → CO₂ (acidic); <strong>phosphorus</strong> burns with a yellow flame → P₄O₁₀ (acidic). Metals: <strong>sodium</strong> yellow flame → Na₂O (basic); <strong>magnesium</strong> brilliant <strong>white</strong> flame → MgO (basic); <strong>calcium</strong> red flame → CaO (basic); <strong>iron</strong> gives <strong>yellow sparks</strong> → iron oxide (basic). <strong>Copper does not burn</strong> — it <strong>turns black</strong> (copper(II) oxide, CuO, basic). There is no \"black flame\".</p></div>",0,""],["n-c12-law-general-solubility-rules-f","chem-law",12,"General solubility rules for salts","<p>(a) <strong>Sodium, potassium and ammonium salts are soluble.</strong> (b) <strong>Nitrates are soluble.</strong> (c) <strong>Chlorides are soluble, except lead and silver.</strong> (d) <strong>Sulfates are soluble, except barium, calcium and lead.</strong> (e) <strong>Carbonates are insoluble, except sodium, potassium and ammonium.</strong> (f) <strong>Hydroxides are insoluble, except sodium, potassium, ammonium and calcium (partially).</strong><br />\nCorrections to your table: the nitrate ion is <strong>NO₃⁻</strong> (charge 1−, not 2−); the soluble carbonate is <strong>ammonium (NH₄⁺)</strong> carbonate, not \"NH₃\". The hydroxide rule (f) was missing.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-a-soluble-salt-f","chem-react",12,"Preparing a soluble salt from an insoluble reactant — excess metal, base or carbonate","<ol>\n<li><strong>Warm the acid</strong> and add the solid (metal, insoluble base or carbonate) <strong>a little at a time until it is in excess</strong> (no more dissolves / no more fizzing) — this makes sure <strong>all the acid is used up</strong>. 2. <strong>Filter</strong> to remove the excess solid. 3. <strong>Heat the filtrate</strong> to evaporate some water until it is <strong>saturated</strong> (crystals form on a glass rod dipped in and cooled). 4. <strong>Leave to cool and crystallise.</strong> 5. <strong>Filter</strong> off the crystals, <strong>wash</strong> with a little cold distilled water and <strong>dry</strong> between filter papers (or in a warm place).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>e.g. copper(II) sulfate: CuO(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → CuSO₄(aq) + H₂O(l); zinc chloride from zinc + HCl; magnesium sulfate from MgCO₃ + H₂SO₄.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-a-soluble-salt-f-2","chem-react",12,"Preparing a soluble salt from an alkali — titration method","<p>Used for <strong>sodium, potassium and ammonium salts</strong> (the reactants are all soluble, so excess can't be filtered off). 1. Use a <strong>volumetric pipette</strong> to put a known volume of alkali in a conical flask with a few drops of <strong>indicator</strong> (methyl orange or thymolphthalein). 2. Add acid from a <strong>burette</strong> until the indicator <strong>just changes colour</strong> (end-point). Note the volume. 3. <strong>Repeat without the indicator</strong> using the same volumes (so the salt is pure). 4. Evaporate and crystallise as above. e.g. NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-an-insoluble-sal","chem-react",12,"Preparing an insoluble salt — precipitation","<ol>\n<li><strong>Mix solutions of two soluble salts</strong> that between them contain the ions of the insoluble salt (e.g. barium chloride + sodium sulfate → barium sulfate). 2. <strong>Filter</strong> to collect the precipitate. 3. <strong>Wash</strong> it with distilled water (removes soluble impurities). 4. <strong>Dry</strong> it (warm oven / between filter papers).</li>\n</ol>",1,""],["n-c12-pro-calcium-hydroxide-is-only","chem-prop",12,"How soluble is calcium hydroxide?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Calcium hydroxide is only partially soluble</p><p>Limewater (aqueous <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong>) is only a <strong>weak, dilute</strong> alkaline solution because calcium hydroxide is <strong>only slightly soluble</strong> (about 1 g/dm³). (Calcium <strong>sulfate</strong> is also only slightly soluble — that's why it is listed as an exception in rule (d).)</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c12-pro-choosing-a-method","chem-prop",12,"Choosing a method","<p>Is the salt <strong>insoluble</strong>? → <strong>precipitation</strong>. Soluble salt of <strong>Na, K or NH₄</strong>? → <strong>titration</strong>. Other soluble salt? → acid + <strong>excess</strong> insoluble metal / base / carbonate, then filter.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-fac-calcium-carbonate-caco-occ","chem-facts",12,"Calcium carbonate occurs naturally as…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) occurs as limestone, marble and chalk</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Limestone is used in the blast furnace (Ch 16) and to make calcium oxide.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c12-fac-colours-of-some-salts","chem-facts",12,"Colours of some salts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Colours of some salts</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Hydrated copper(II) sulfate CuSO₄·5H₂O is blue</strong> (anhydrous: <strong>white</strong>); <strong>hydrated cobalt(II) chloride CoCl₂·6H₂O is pink</strong> (anhydrous: <strong>blue</strong>); <strong>iron(II) sulfate</strong> (FeSO₄) is green; <strong>lead(II) iodide PbI₂</strong> is a <strong>yellow precipitate</strong>; Group I, Group II and zinc salts are white. Your formulae \"Fe₂SO₄\", \"Co₂Cl₂\" and \"lead₂ iodide\" were wrong — iron(II) sulfate is <strong>FeSO₄</strong> and cobalt(II) chloride is <strong>CoCl₂</strong>, because Fe²⁺/Co²⁺ need only one SO₄²⁻ or two Cl⁻.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c13-law-group-names","chem-law",13,"Group names","<p><strong>Group I — alkali metals</strong> (Li, Na, K…). Group II — alkaline earth metals. <strong>Group VII — halogens</strong> (Cl, Br, I…). <strong>Group VIII — noble gases</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-law-transition-elements-lie-be","chem-law",13,"Where are the transition elements in the Periodic Table?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Transition elements lie between Group II and Group III</p><p>They are all metals.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c13-law-patterns-you-can-use-to-pr","chem-law",13,"Patterns you can use to predict properties","<p><strong>Across a period</strong>: elements change from <strong>metals to non-metals</strong> (left to right). <strong>Group number → charge of ion</strong>: Group I <strong>+1</strong>, II <strong>+2</strong>, III <strong>+3</strong>, V <strong>−3</strong>, VI <strong>−2</strong>, VII <strong>−1</strong>. <strong>Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties because they have the same number of outer-shell electrons</strong> (same outer electronic configuration). Use the position (group, metal/non-metal) and the trends to <strong>predict</strong> properties of unfamiliar elements.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-rea-alkali-metals-cold-water-m","chem-react",13,"Alkali metals + cold water: products and observations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alkali metals + cold water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</p><p>e.g. <strong>2Na(s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)</strong>. Observations: <strong>floats, fizzes (bubbles), moves around, melts into a ball</strong> (Na, K), <strong>gets smaller and disappears</strong>; <strong>potassium</strong> also burns with a <strong>lilac</strong> flame; the solution becomes <strong>alkaline</strong> (universal indicator → purple). Reactivity: <strong>Li &lt; Na &lt; K</strong>.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c13-rea-chlorine-potassium-iodide","chem-react",13,"Chlorine + potassium iodide","<p><strong>Cl₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + I₂(aq)</strong> — solution turns from colourless to <strong>brown</strong> (iodine). Chlorine is more reactive than iodine so it displaces it. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Ionic: Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-dif-group-i-alkali-metals-vs-g","chem-diff",13,"Group I (alkali metals) vs Group VII (halogens)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Group I</th><th>Group VII</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Type</td><td><strong>Metals</strong> (soft — cut with a knife)</td><td><strong>Non-metals</strong>, <strong>diatomic</strong> molecules (Cl₂, Br₂, I₂)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Outer electrons</td><td>1</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ion formed</td><td><strong>+1</strong> (lose 1 electron)</td><td><strong>−1</strong> (gain 1 electron)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Melting point down the group</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong></td><td>Increases</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Density down the group</td><td><strong>Increases</strong></td><td><strong>Increases</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Reactivity down the group</strong></td><td><strong>Increases</strong> (outer electron lost more easily)</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong> (electron gained less easily)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appearance at r.t.p.</td><td>Shiny, silvery when freshly cut</td><td><strong>Chlorine: pale yellow-green gas; bromine: red-brown liquid; iodine: grey-black solid</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c13-pro-most-reactive-metal-and-no","chem-prop",13,"Most reactive metal and non-metal","<p>Reactivity of metals increases <strong>down</strong> groups and to the <strong>left</strong>; reactivity of non-metals increases <strong>up</strong> Group VII. So the most reactive metal you will meet is <strong>caesium</strong> (low down in Group I; francium is radioactive and very rare) and the most reactive non-metal is <strong>fluorine</strong> (top of Group VII).</p>",0,""],["n-c13-pro-why-group-i-metals-are-sto","chem-prop",13,"Why Group I metals are stored in oil","<p>They are <strong>so reactive</strong> that they react quickly with <strong>oxygen and water vapour in the air</strong>; oil keeps air and water away.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-pro-common-properties-of-halog","chem-prop",13,"Common properties of halogens, noble gases and transition metals","<p><strong>Halogens:</strong> non-metals, diatomic, coloured, poisonous, react with metals to form halides, density increases and reactivity decreases down the group. <strong>Noble gases:</strong> colourless, monatomic, unreactive (full outer shell). <strong>Transition metals:</strong> high density, high m.p., coloured compounds, catalysts, <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> variable oxidation numbers.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-pro-predicting-properties","chem-prop",13,"Predicting properties","<p>Continue the trend: e.g. <strong>rubidium</strong> (below K) has a lower m.p., higher density and reacts <strong>more violently</strong> with water than potassium; <strong>astatine</strong> (below iodine) is a darker solid, denser and <strong>less reactive</strong> than iodine (it can't displace iodine).</p>",1,""],["n-c13-fac-periodic-trends-summary","chem-facts",13,"Summary of the Periodic Table trends?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Periodic trends summary</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Across a period:</strong> metallic → non-metallic. <strong>Down Group I:</strong> m.p./b.p. <strong>decrease</strong>, metals get <strong>softer</strong>, density <strong>increases</strong>, reactivity <strong>increases</strong>. <strong>Down Group VII:</strong> density and m.p. <strong>increase</strong> (gas → liquid → solid), reactivity <strong>decreases</strong>. <strong>Down any group:</strong> atoms get larger and more metallic.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c14-rea-metals-oxygen-air-metal-ox","chem-react",14,"Metals + oxygen: product and examples?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metals + oxygen (air) → metal oxide</p><p>e.g. 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (bright white flame); 2Cu + O₂ → 2CuO (turns black, no flame). Metal oxides are <strong>basic</strong>.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c14-rea-metals-water-steam","chem-react",14,"Metals + water/steam","<p><strong>Reactive metal + cold water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</strong>: 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂; Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂. <strong>Less reactive metal + steam → metal oxide + hydrogen</strong>: Mg + H₂O(g) → MgO + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-rea-metals-dilute-acids-salt-h","chem-react",14,"Metals + dilute acids: products and observations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metals + dilute acids → salt + hydrogen</p><p>e.g. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂; Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂. Fizzing; gas pops with a lighted splint. <strong>Copper, silver and gold do not react</strong> (below hydrogen in the reactivity series).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c14-law-composition-of-alloys","chem-law",14,"Composition of alloys","<p><strong>Brass = copper + zinc.</strong> <strong>Stainless steel = iron + chromium, nickel and carbon.</strong> These two are the ones the syllabus names. Bronze (Cu + Sn), mild steel (Fe + C) and solder (Sn + Pb) are not required.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-dif-physical-properties-of-met","chem-diff",14,"Physical properties of metals vs non-metals","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Property</th><th>Metals</th><th>Non-metals</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Thermal conductivity</strong></td><td><strong>Good</strong></td><td><strong>Poor</strong> (insulators)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Electrical conductivity</strong></td><td><strong>Good</strong></td><td><strong>Poor</strong> (except graphite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Malleability and ductility</strong></td><td><strong>Malleable and ductile</strong></td><td><strong>Brittle</strong> when solid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Melting and boiling points</strong></td><td>Usually <strong>high</strong></td><td>Usually <strong>low</strong> (except C, Si)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appearance / state</td><td>Shiny; solids at r.t.p. (except mercury)</td><td>Dull; many are gases at r.t.p.</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c14-pro-general-properties-of-meta","chem-prop",14,"General properties of metals","<p><strong>Physical:</strong> good conductors of heat and electricity, malleable, ductile, usually high m.p./b.p., shiny, high density. <strong>Chemical:</strong> react with <strong>oxygen</strong> to form basic oxides, with <strong>water or steam</strong> (reactive metals) and with <strong>dilute acids</strong> to give salts and hydrogen; they form <strong>positive ions</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-pro-water-and-steam","chem-prop",14,"Water and steam","<p><strong>Metal + cold water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</strong> (K, Na, Ca). <strong>Metal + steam → metal oxide + hydrogen</strong> (Mg, Zn, Fe).</p>",0,""],["n-c14-pro-why-alloys-are-harder-and","chem-prop",14,"Why alloys are harder and stronger","<p>In a pure metal the ions are all the same size and arranged in <strong>layers that slide over each other easily</strong> (soft, malleable). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>In an alloy the different-sized atoms distort the layers, so the layers can no longer slide over each other</strong> → the alloy is <strong>harder and stronger</strong>. Alloys are also designed for resistance to corrosion (stainless steel).</p>",1,""],["n-c14-use-aluminium","chem-uses",14,"Aluminium","<p><strong>Aircraft</strong> — <strong>low density</strong>. <strong>Overhead electrical cables</strong> — <strong>low density</strong> and <strong>good electrical conductivity</strong>. <strong>Food containers</strong> — <strong>resistance to corrosion</strong> (protected by its oxide layer).</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-copper","chem-uses",14,"Copper","<p><strong>Electrical wiring</strong> — <strong>good electrical conductivity</strong> and <strong>ductility</strong> (can be drawn into wires).</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-zinc","chem-uses",14,"Zinc","<p><strong>Galvanising</strong> iron and steel to stop rusting (Chapter 16)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-stainless-steel","chem-uses",14,"Stainless steel","<p><strong>Cutlery</strong> — because of its <strong>hardness</strong> and <strong>resistance to rusting</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-fac-an-alloy-is-a-mixture-not","chem-facts",14,"Is an alloy a mixture or a compound?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">An alloy is a mixture, not a compound.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The metals are not chemically combined.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-water","chem-react",15,"Metals with water","<p><strong>Potassium, sodium and calcium react with cold water</strong> → metal hydroxide + hydrogen. <strong>K</strong> — very vigorous, melts, lilac flame; <strong>Na</strong> — vigorous, fizzes, melts into a ball, moves on the surface; <strong>Ca</strong> — steady fizzing, sinks, cloudy solution (Ca(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble). <strong>Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold water but quickly with steam</strong>: Mg + H₂O(g) → MgO + H₂ (white glow; white solid).</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-air-oxygen","chem-react",15,"Metals with air/oxygen","<p>K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe burn (more reactive metals more vigorously) to form oxides; copper only forms a black oxide layer; silver and gold do not react.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-dilute-hydroch","chem-react",15,"Metals with dilute hydrochloric acid","<p><strong>Magnesium</strong> — very fast fizzing; <strong>zinc</strong> — moderate; <strong>iron</strong> — slow; <strong>copper, silver, gold — no reaction</strong> (below hydrogen). e.g. Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-dilute-sulfuri","chem-react",15,"Metals with dilute sulfuric acid","<p>Same pattern as hydrochloric acid; the salt is a <strong>sulfate</strong>, e.g. Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-the-silver-tree-displaceme","chem-react",15,"The \"silver tree\" displacement","<p>A strip of <strong>copper</strong> in <strong>silver nitrate</strong> solution: <strong>Cu(s) + 2AgNO₃(aq) → Cu(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2Ag(s)</strong> — crystals of silver grow on the copper and the solution turns <strong>blue</strong>. Shows copper is more reactive than silver.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-pro-explaining-reactions-using","chem-prop",15,"Explaining reactions using the reactivity series","<p>Metals <strong>above hydrogen</strong> react with dilute acids to give hydrogen; metals <strong>below hydrogen</strong> (Cu, Ag, Au) do not. The <strong>higher</strong> the metal, the <strong>more vigorous</strong> the reaction with water, steam and acids.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-pro-deducing-an-order-of-react","chem-prop",15,"Deducing an order of reactivity","<p>From results (e.g. rate of bubbling with acid, temperature rise, which displacement reactions happen): the metal that <strong>displaces the most others</strong>, or reacts fastest, is the most reactive.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-pro-apparent-unreactivity-of-a","chem-prop",15,"Apparent unreactivity of aluminium","<p>Aluminium is high in the series but seems unreactive because it is covered by a <strong>thin, tough layer of aluminium oxide</strong> that <strong>stops air and water reaching the metal underneath</strong>. (This is why it is used for food containers.)</p>",1,""],["n-c15-fac-the-reactivity-series","chem-facts",15,"The reactivity series?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The reactivity series</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, aluminium, (carbon), zinc, iron, (hydrogen), copper, silver, gold</strong> — most → least reactive. This is the exact syllabus list. Your list also had tin and lead (between iron and hydrogen) — correct, but not required. Memory aid: <strong>P</strong>lease <strong>S</strong>end <strong>C</strong>harlie's <strong>M</strong>onkeys <strong>A</strong>nd <strong>C</strong>ute <strong>Z</strong>ebras <strong>I</strong>nto <strong>H</strong>ot <strong>C</strong>ountries <strong>S</strong>igned <strong>G</strong>ordon.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c15-fac-carbon-and-hydrogen-are-no","chem-facts",15,"Why are carbon and hydrogen in the reactivity series?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carbon and hydrogen are non-metals included for comparison.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Metals below carbon can be extracted by heating with carbon (Ch 16).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c16-rea-extraction-of-iron-in-the","chem-react",16,"Extraction of iron in the blast furnace","<p>Raw materials: <strong>hematite, coke (carbon), limestone</strong> (and hot <strong>air</strong>).<br />\n(a) <strong>Burning coke</strong> provides <strong>heat</strong> and makes carbon dioxide: <strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong>.<br />\n(b) <strong>Carbon dioxide is reduced</strong> to carbon monoxide by more coke: <strong>C + CO₂ → 2CO</strong>.<br />\n(c) <strong>Carbon monoxide reduces iron(III) oxide</strong> to iron: <strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong>. Molten iron collects at the bottom.<br />\n(d) <strong>Limestone thermally decomposes</strong>: <strong>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</strong>.<br />\n(e) <strong>Slag forms</strong>: <strong>CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃</strong>.<br />\n(Symbol equations are Supplement; Core students describe the steps in words.)</p>",1,""],["n-c16-rea-extraction-of-aluminium-by","chem-react",16,"Extraction of aluminium by electrolysis","<p><strong>Aluminium is extracted from purified bauxite (aluminium oxide) by electrolysis</strong> — it is too reactive to be reduced by carbon. Electrolyte: <strong>aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite</strong>; <strong>graphite (carbon) electrodes</strong>.<br />\n<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Role of cryolite:</strong> it <strong>lowers the melting point</strong> of the electrolyte (from about 2000 °C to about 950 °C), saving energy, and improves conductivity.<br />\n<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Cathode (−):</strong> <strong>Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al</strong> (molten aluminium collects at the bottom). <strong>Anode (+):</strong> <strong>2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻</strong>.<br />\n(Details of purifying bauxite are not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-c16-law-the-carbon-anodes-must-be","chem-law",16,"The carbon anodes must be replaced regularly","<p>At the high temperature the <strong>oxygen made at the anodes reacts with the carbon</strong> anodes to form <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> (C + O₂ → CO₂), so the anodes <strong>burn away</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c16-law-least-reactive-metals-copp","chem-law",16,"Least reactive metals — copper, silver, gold","<p>These are the easiest to obtain; <strong>gold and silver are found uncombined</strong> (as the element) because they are so unreactive.</p>",0,""],["n-c16-law-ease-of-extraction-depends","chem-law",16,"Ease of extraction depends on reactivity","<p><strong>Metals high in the reactivity series</strong> (K, Na, Ca, Mg, <strong>Al</strong>) form very stable compounds — extracted by <strong>electrolysis</strong> of the molten compound (expensive: much electricity). <strong>Metals below carbon</strong> (Zn, <strong>Fe</strong>, Pb, Cu) are extracted by <strong>heating the oxide with carbon</strong> (reduction). <strong>Unreactive metals</strong> (Ag, Au) are found <strong>uncombined</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c16-pro-carbon-and-carbon-monoxide","chem-prop",16,"What do carbon and carbon monoxide do to metal oxides?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carbon (and carbon monoxide) is a reducing agent</p><p>It removes oxygen from metal oxides (and is itself oxidised to CO₂).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c16-pro-the-main-ore-of-iron-is-he","chem-prop",16,"Main ore of iron: name and formula?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The main ore of iron is hematite, Fe₂O₃</p><p>(Iron(III) oxide.)</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c16-pro-the-main-impurity-in-hemat","chem-prop",16,"Main impurity in hematite?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The main impurity in hematite is silicon(IV) oxide, SiO₂ (sand)</p><p>Removed as slag by the limestone.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c16-pro-stages-in-getting-a-metal","chem-prop",16,"Stages in getting a metal","<p>Not \"mining, purification, electrolysis\" for every metal: 1. <strong>mining</strong> the ore; 2. <strong>concentrating/purifying</strong> the ore; 3. <strong>reducing</strong> the metal compound — <strong>by carbon</strong> (iron, zinc) <strong>or by electrolysis</strong> (aluminium and more reactive metals); 4. refining the metal if very pure metal is needed.</p>",0,""],["n-c16-pro-the-main-ore-of-aluminium","chem-prop",16,"Main ore of aluminium?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The main ore of aluminium is bauxite</p><p><strong>Cryolite is not the ore</strong> — it is the <strong>solvent</strong> (sodium aluminium fluoride, Na₃AlF₆) used to lower the melting point of the electrolyte.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c16-pro-purified-bauxite-is-alumin","chem-prop",16,"What is purified bauxite?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Purified bauxite is aluminium oxide (alumina), Al₂O₃</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c16-pro-methods-of-preventing-rust","chem-prop",16,"Methods of preventing rust","<p><strong>Barrier methods</strong> — <strong>painting, greasing (oil), coating with plastic</strong> — work by <strong>excluding oxygen and water</strong> from the iron. <strong>Galvanising</strong> (zinc coating) — barrier + sacrificial <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>. <strong>Sacrificial protection</strong> — blocks of zinc or magnesium <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",1,""],["n-c16-fac-rust-is-hydrated-iron-iii","chem-facts",16,"What is rust, and which metals rust?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide; only iron and steel rust.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Other metals corrode but do not \"rust\".</p></div>",0,""],["n-c16-fac-in-the-blast-furnace-carbo","chem-facts",16,"Main reducing agent in the blast furnace?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In the blast furnace, carbon monoxide is the main reducing agent.</p>",0,""],["n-c17-exp-greenhouse-gases-and-globa","chem-expl",17,"Greenhouse gases and global warming","<p><strong>Carbon dioxide and methane</strong> are the greenhouse gases in the syllabus. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> How they cause global warming: (a) the Earth's surface <strong>absorbs</strong> energy from the Sun and <strong>emits thermal (infrared) energy</strong>; greenhouse gases <strong>absorb</strong> some of this thermal energy and <strong>re-emit/reflect</strong> it back towards Earth; (b) this <strong>reduces thermal energy loss to space</strong>, so the Earth warms. Higher levels → <strong>increased global warming → climate change</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c17-pro-carbon-dioxide-is-an-air-p","chem-prop",17,"Carbon dioxide as an air pollutant: source and effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carbon dioxide is an air pollutant</p><p>From the <strong>complete combustion of carbon-containing fuels</strong>; higher levels cause <strong>increased global warming, leading to climate change</strong>.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c17-pro-greenhouse-gases","chem-prop",17,"Greenhouse gases","<p>The syllabus only requires <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> and <strong>methane</strong>. (Water vapour, nitrous oxide, sulfur hexafluoride and CFCs are also greenhouse gases but are not required.)</p>",0,""],["n-c17-pro-some-metal-compounds-in-wa","chem-prop",17,"Which metal compounds in water are toxic?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Some metal compounds in water are toxic</p><p>e.g. compounds of <strong>lead</strong> and <strong>mercury</strong> (heavy metals) are poisonous — they damage the body (lead: kidneys and liver; mercury: nervous system).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c17-pro-composition-of-clean-dry-a","chem-prop",17,"Composition of clean, dry air","<p><strong>About 78% nitrogen (N₂), 21% oxygen (O₂), the remainder a mixture of noble gases (mostly argon) and carbon dioxide (CO₂, about 0.04%).</strong></p>",0,""],["n-c17-pro-air-pollutants-sources-and","chem-prop",17,"Air pollutants: sources and effects","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Pollutant</th><th>Source</th><th>Adverse effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong></td><td><strong>Complete combustion</strong> of carbon-containing fuels</td><td><strong>Increased global warming → climate change</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong></td><td><strong>Incomplete combustion</strong> of carbon-containing fuels</td><td><strong>Toxic</strong> gas (stops blood carrying oxygen)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Particulates</strong> (soot)</td><td><strong>Incomplete combustion</strong> of carbon-containing fuels</td><td><strong>Respiratory problems and cancer</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Methane</strong></td><td><strong>Decomposition of vegetation</strong> and <strong>waste gases from digestion in animals</strong> (cattle)</td><td><strong>Increased global warming → climate change</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Oxides of nitrogen</strong></td><td><strong>Car engines</strong></td><td><strong>Acid rain, photochemical smog, respiratory problems</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfur dioxide</strong></td><td><strong>Combustion of fossil fuels containing sulfur compounds</strong></td><td><strong>Acid rain</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c17-pro-how-oxides-of-nitrogen-for","chem-prop",17,"How oxides of nitrogen form in car engines","<p>At the <strong>high temperature</strong> inside the engine, <strong>nitrogen and oxygen from the air react</strong>: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO (then 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂). They are removed by catalytic converters.</p>",1,""],["n-c17-pro-strategies-to-reduce-the-e","chem-prop",17,"Strategies to reduce the effects","<p><strong>Climate change:</strong> <strong>planting trees</strong>, <strong>reducing livestock farming</strong>, <strong>decreasing use of fossil fuels</strong>, <strong>increasing use of hydrogen and renewable energy</strong> (e.g. wind, solar). <strong>Acid rain:</strong> <strong>catalytic converters</strong> in vehicles; <strong>low-sulfur fuels</strong>; <strong>flue gas desulfurisation with calcium oxide</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c17-pro-photosynthesis","chem-prop",17,"Photosynthesis","<p><strong>The reaction between carbon dioxide and water to produce glucose and oxygen in the presence of chlorophyll and using energy from light.</strong> Word: <strong>carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Symbol: <strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong>. It removes CO₂ from the air — why planting trees helps.</p>",1,""],["n-c17-pro-substances-in-natural-wate","chem-prop",17,"Substances in natural water","<p>Water from natural sources may contain: <strong>dissolved oxygen, metal compounds, plastics, sewage, harmful microbes, nitrates from fertilisers, phosphates from fertilisers and detergents</strong>. <strong>Beneficial:</strong> dissolved oxygen for aquatic life; some metal compounds provide essential minerals. <strong>Harmful:</strong> some metal compounds are toxic; some plastics harm aquatic life; sewage contains harmful microbes that cause disease; <strong>nitrates and phosphates lead to deoxygenation of water and damage to aquatic life</strong> (details of eutrophication not required).</p>",0,""],["n-c17-pro-why-distilled-water-is-use","chem-prop",17,"Why distilled water is used in practical chemistry","<p>It <strong>contains fewer chemical impurities</strong> than tap water, which could react or interfere with results.</p>",0,""],["n-c17-fac-complete-combustion-co-h-o","chem-facts",17,"Products of complete and incomplete combustion?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Complete combustion → CO₂ + H₂O; incomplete combustion → CO and/or C (soot) + H₂O.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Incomplete combustion happens when oxygen is limited.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c18-exp-naming-prefixes-and-ending","chem-expl",18,"Naming: prefixes and endings","<p>Number of carbons: <strong>meth- 1, eth- 2, prop- 3, but- 4</strong>. Endings: <strong>-ane</strong> alkane, <strong>-ene</strong> alkene, <strong>-ol</strong> alcohol, <strong>-oic acid</strong> carboxylic acid. Position numbers for C=C and –OH: <strong>but-1-ene / but-2-ene; propan-1-ol / propan-2-ol; butan-1-ol / butan-2-ol</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alk","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alkanes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. propane C₃H₈.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alk-2","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alkenes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethene C₂H₄.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alc","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alcohols","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethanol C₂H₅OH.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-car","chem-form",18,"General formula of the carboxylic acids","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethanoic acid CH₃COOH. Here n counts the carbons before COOH, so ethanoic acid has n = 1.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-dif-molecular-formula-vs-displ","chem-diff",18,"Molecular formula vs displayed formula","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Molecular formula</th><th>Displayed formula</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shows</td><td><strong>Number and type of atoms</strong> in one molecule</td><td><strong>All atoms and all bonds</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example (ethanol)</td><td>C₂H₆O</td><td>Every C–H, C–C, C–O and O–H bond drawn</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shows isomers apart?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c18-dif-structural-formula-vs-empi","chem-diff",18,"Structural formula vs empirical formula","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Structural formula</th><th>Empirical formula</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shows</td><td><strong>How the atoms are arranged</strong> (groups in order)</td><td><strong>Simplest whole-number ratio</strong> of atoms</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example (butane)</td><td>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃</td><td><strong>C₂H₅</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example (ethene)</td><td>CH₂=CH₂</td><td>CH₂</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c18-pro-covalent-bonding-of-carbon","chem-prop",18,"Covalent bonding of carbon","<p><strong>Each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds</strong>; carbon atoms can join to each other in <strong>chains</strong> and form <strong>double bonds</strong> (C=C). H forms <strong>1</strong> bond, O forms <strong>2</strong>, Cl forms <strong>1</strong>. Use these numbers to check displayed formulae.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-pro-the-simplest-organic-compo","chem-prop",18,"What are the simplest organic compounds?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The simplest organic compounds are hydrocarbons</p><p>The simplest of all is <strong>methane, CH₄</strong> (main constituent of natural gas).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c18-pro-names-and-displayed-formul","chem-prop",18,"Names and displayed formulae you must draw","<p>Core: <strong>methane, ethane, ethene, ethanol, ethanoic acid</strong> and the products of reactions in Ch 19. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> All <strong>unbranched</strong> alkanes, alkenes, alcohols and carboxylic acids <strong>up to four carbon atoms</strong>, and their esters.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Series</th><th>1 C</th><th>2 C</th><th>3 C</th><th>4 C</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Alkane</td><td>methane CH₄</td><td>ethane C₂H₆</td><td>propane C₃H₈</td><td>butane C₄H₁₀</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alkene</td><td>—</td><td>ethene C₂H₄</td><td>propene C₃H₆</td><td>but-1-ene / but-2-ene C₄H₈</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>methanol CH₃OH</td><td>ethanol C₂H₅OH</td><td>propan-1-ol / propan-2-ol</td><td>butan-1-ol / butan-2-ol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carboxylic acid</td><td>methanoic acid HCOOH</td><td>ethanoic acid CH₃COOH</td><td>propanoic acid C₂H₅COOH</td><td>butanoic acid C₃H₇COOH</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c18-fac-don-t-confuse-isomer-same","chem-facts",18,"Isomer compared with isotope?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Don't confuse isomer (same formula, different structure) with isotope (same element, different neutrons).</p>",0,""],["n-c18-fac-ane-all-single-bonds-satur","chem-facts",18,"‑ane and ‑ene: which bonds?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">\"‑ane\" = all single bonds (saturated); \"‑ene\" = C=C (unsaturated).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-cracking-decane","chem-react",19,"Cracking decane","<p>e.g. <strong>C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄</strong> (octane for petrol + ethene); or C₁₀H₂₂ → C₅H₁₂ + C₂H₄ + C₃H₆. Check: carbons and hydrogens balance; at least one product is an alkene. Conditions: <strong>high temperature (≈ 500 °C) and a catalyst</strong> (aluminium oxide / silica).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-making-ethanol-industriall","chem-react",19,"Making ethanol industrially — hydration of ethene","<p><strong>C₂H₄(g) + H₂O(g) → C₂H₅OH(g)</strong> at <strong>300 °C, 60 atm (6000 kPa), acid catalyst</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-making-ethanoic-acid","chem-react",19,"Making ethanoic acid","<p>(a) <strong>Oxidation of ethanol with acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong> (warm): C₂H₅OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O — the manganate(VII) turns <strong>purple → colourless</strong>. (b) <strong>Bacterial oxidation during vinegar production</strong>: ethanol in wine/beer is oxidised by bacteria in air to ethanoic acid.</p>",1,""],["n-c19-rea-combustion-of-alkanes","chem-react",19,"Combustion of alkanes","<p>Alkanes are <strong>generally unreactive, except for combustion and substitution by chlorine</strong>. <strong>Complete combustion</strong> (plenty of oxygen) → <strong>carbon dioxide + water</strong>: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. <strong>Incomplete combustion</strong> (limited oxygen) → <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> and/or <strong>carbon (soot)</strong> + water.</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-combustion-of-ethanol","chem-react",19,"Combustion of ethanol","<p>Ethanol burns with a clean blue flame: <strong>C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</strong> (exothermic — used as a fuel).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-reactions-of-ethanoic-acid","chem-react",19,"Reactions of ethanoic acid","<p>It is a <strong>weak acid</strong> with the usual acid reactions; the salts are <strong>ethanoates</strong> (CH₃COO⁻). With <strong>metals</strong>: 2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂ (<strong>magnesium ethanoate</strong>; slow fizzing). With <strong>bases</strong>: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O (<strong>sodium ethanoate</strong>). With <strong>carbonates</strong>: 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂ (fizzing).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-dif-ethanol-by-fermentation-vs","chem-diff",19,"Ethanol by fermentation vs by catalytic hydration of ethene","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fermentation</th><th>Catalytic addition of steam to ethene</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Raw material</td><td><strong>Sugar/glucose from crops — renewable</strong></td><td><strong>Ethene from petroleum (cracking) — non-renewable</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conditions</td><td><strong>25–35 °C</strong>, yeast, no oxygen, normal pressure</td><td><strong>300 °C, 60 atm</strong>, acid catalyst</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rate</td><td><strong>Slow</strong></td><td><strong>Fast</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Process</td><td><strong>Batch</strong></td><td><strong>Continuous</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Product</td><td><strong>Impure</strong> (≈ 15% ethanol) — needs distillation</td><td><strong>Pure</strong> ethanol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy cost</td><td><strong>Low</strong> (low temperature)</td><td><strong>High</strong> (high temperature and pressure)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other</td><td>CO₂ released; uses farmland</td><td>Can't continue when petroleum runs out</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c19-pro-addition-polymers-from-alk","chem-prop",19,"Addition polymers from alkenes","<p><strong>Ethene → poly(ethene); propene → poly(propene)</strong> — the C=C opens and the monomers join in a long chain. Butene does <strong>not</strong> make synthetic rubber — that is made from buta-1,3-diene (and isn't in the syllabus).</p>",1,""],["n-c19-pro-fermentation-works-best-at","chem-prop",19,"Fermentation works best at 25–35 °C","<p>Too cold → yeast enzymes too slow; too hot → enzymes <strong>denatured</strong> and yeast killed. Also needs <strong>no oxygen</strong> (otherwise the ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-use-uses-of-ethanol","chem-uses",19,"Uses of ethanol","<p><strong>(a) A solvent</strong> (e.g. in perfumes, inks, medicines). <strong>(b) A fuel</strong> (burns cleanly; can be mixed with petrol).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-fac-alkanes-substitution-with","chem-facts",19,"Typical reactions of alkanes and of alkenes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alkanes: substitution (with Cl₂ in UV). Alkenes: addition.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c19-fac-bromine-water-orange-colou","chem-facts",19,"Bromine water test for C=C?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bromine water: orange → colourless = C=C present.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c20-use-petroleum-fractions-and-th","chem-uses",20,"Petroleum fractions and their uses","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Fraction (top → bottom)</th><th>Use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Refinery gas</strong></td><td><strong>Gas used in heating and cooking</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Gasoline / petrol</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in cars</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Naphtha</strong></td><td><strong>Chemical feedstock</strong> (to make other chemicals, e.g. by cracking)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Kerosene / paraffin</strong></td><td><strong>Jet fuel</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Diesel oil / gas oil</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in diesel engines</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Fuel oil</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in ships and home heating systems</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Lubricating oil</strong></td><td><strong>Lubricants, waxes and polishes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Bitumen</strong></td><td><strong>Making roads</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(Carbon-chain lengths and boiling temperatures are not required.)</p>",0,""],["n-c20-pro-the-fossil-fuels-are-coal","chem-prop",20,"Name the fossil fuels.","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The fossil fuels are coal, natural gas and petroleum</p><p>Formed from the remains of plants and animals over millions of years; <strong>non-renewable</strong> (finite).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c20-pro-natural-polymers-proteins","chem-prop",20,"Natural polymers — proteins","<p><strong>Proteins are natural polyamides</strong>, formed from <strong>amino acid monomers</strong> (general structure H₂N–CHR–COOH, where R represents different side-chains), joined by <strong>amide (peptide) links –CO–NH–</strong>. Enzymes are proteins; DNA is a natural polymer too but is not required here.</p>",1,""],["n-c20-pro-deducing-addition-polymers","chem-prop",20,"Deducing addition polymers","<p>Take the alkene, <strong>open the C=C</strong>, draw the <strong>repeat unit</strong> in brackets with <strong>bonds sticking out</strong> and <strong>n</strong> outside. <strong>Ethene CH₂=CH₂ → poly(ethene) –[CH₂–CH₂]ₙ–</strong>; <strong>propene CH₃CH=CH₂ → poly(propene)</strong>, repeat unit –CH₂–CH(CH₃)–; <strong>chloroethene CH₂=CHCl → poly(chloroethene) (PVC)</strong>, repeat unit –CH₂–CHCl–. Reverse it to find the monomer: the 2 carbons of the repeat unit joined by C=C.</p>",1,""],["n-c20-pro-properties-of-the-fraction","chem-prop",20,"Properties of the fractions — bottom to top of the column","<p>Going <strong>up</strong> the column the fractions have: <strong>(a) decreasing chain length</strong>, <strong>(b) higher volatility</strong>, <strong>(c) lower boiling points</strong>, <strong>(d) lower viscosity</strong> (runnier).</p>",0,""],["n-c20-pro-plastics-disposal-and-envi","chem-prop",20,"Plastics: disposal and environmental challenges","<p>Because most plastics are <strong>non-biodegradable</strong> and <strong>unreactive</strong>: <strong>(a) landfill sites</strong> fill up and the plastic stays for hundreds of years; <strong>(b) they accumulate in oceans</strong>, harming aquatic life (animals swallow or get trapped in them); <strong>(c) burning them forms toxic gases</strong> (e.g. HCl from PVC, CO). Recycling (e.g. PET) reduces these problems.</p>",0,""],["n-c20-pro-deducing-condensation-poly","chem-prop",20,"Deducing condensation polymers","<p>Show the two monomers as blocks with their end groups; join –COOH to –NH₂ (→ <strong>amide link</strong> –CONH–) or –COOH to –OH (→ <strong>ester link</strong> –COO–), removing <strong>H₂O</strong> each time. The repeat unit contains <strong>one of each monomer</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c20-dif-addition-vs-condensation-p","chem-diff",20,"Addition vs condensation polymerisation","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Addition polymerisation</th><th>Condensation polymerisation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Monomers</td><td><strong>Alkenes</strong> (contain <strong>C=C</strong>)</td><td>Two different monomers each with <strong>two functional groups</strong> (e.g. dicarboxylic acid + diamine / diol)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Products</td><td><strong>Only the polymer</strong></td><td>Polymer <strong>+ a small molecule (water)</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Linkage</td><td>C–C chain</td><td><strong>Amide</strong> (–CONH–) or <strong>ester</strong> (–COO–) links</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Poly(ethene), poly(propene)</td><td><strong>Nylon</strong> (polyamide), <strong>PET</strong> (polyester), proteins</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-c20-fac-polymer-questions-draw-the","chem-facts",20,"How do you draw a repeat unit?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Polymer questions: draw the repeat unit with continuation bonds and brackets.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c20-fac-proteins-and-nylon-both-ha","chem-facts",20,"Which link is in proteins and nylon, and which in PET?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Proteins and nylon both have amide links; PET has ester links.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c21-exp-filtration","chem-expl",21,"Filtration","<p>Separates an <strong>insoluble solid</strong> from a <strong>liquid</strong>: the solid (<strong>residue</strong>) stays in the filter paper; the liquid (<strong>filtrate</strong>) passes through.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-exp-crystallisation","chem-expl",21,"Crystallisation","<p>Obtains a <strong>soluble solid</strong> from a <strong>solution</strong>: heat to evaporate some solvent until the solution is <strong>saturated</strong> (crystals appear on a cooled glass rod), then <strong>cool</strong> so crystals form; filter, wash with a little cold solvent, dry.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-exp-simple-distillation","chem-expl",21,"Simple distillation","<p>Separates a <strong>solvent (liquid)</strong> from a <strong>solution</strong>, e.g. pure water from salt water: the liquid <strong>boils</strong>, the vapour is <strong>condensed</strong> in a (Liebig) condenser and collected; the solid stays in the flask.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-exp-fractional-distillation","chem-expl",21,"Fractional distillation","<p>Separates <strong>miscible liquids with different boiling points</strong> (e.g. ethanol and water; petroleum fractions; air): a <strong>fractionating column</strong> is used; the liquid with the <strong>lowest boiling point</strong> reaches the top first and distils over; thermometer reading shows which liquid is coming over.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-exp-using-a-suitable-solvent","chem-expl",21,"Using a suitable solvent","<p>Dissolve one component and not the other, then filter, e.g. <strong>salt + sand</strong>: add water, stir, filter (sand = residue), crystallise the salt from the filtrate. Other solvents (e.g. ethanol) dissolve substances that water can't.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-dif-accuracy-vs-precision","chem-diff",21,"Accuracy vs precision","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Precision</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td><strong>How close a measurement is to the true value</strong></td><td><strong>How close repeated measurements are to each other</strong> (and the size of the smallest scale division read)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Improved by</td><td>Calibrated apparatus, avoiding systematic errors (e.g. heat loss, reading at eye level)</td><td>Using apparatus with <strong>smaller divisions</strong> (burette rather than measuring cylinder); repeating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>A thermometer reading 99.0 °C for boiling water is less accurate than 100.0 °C</td><td>Burette (±0.05 cm³) is more precise than a measuring cylinder (±0.5 cm³)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c21-dif-independent-dependent-and","chem-diff",21,"Independent, dependent and control variables","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Variable</th><th>Independent</th><th>Dependent</th><th>Control</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What happens to it</td><td><strong>Changed</strong> deliberately</td><td><strong>Measured</strong></td><td><strong>Kept the same</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rate example (marble + acid)</td><td>Concentration of acid</td><td>Volume of CO₂ per minute</td><td>Mass and size of marble chips, volume of acid, temperature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Graph axis</td><td>x-axis</td><td>y-axis</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c21-dif-simple-distillation-vs-fra","chem-diff",21,"Simple distillation vs fractional distillation","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Simple distillation</th><th>Fractional distillation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Separates</td><td><strong>A solvent from a solution</strong> (liquid from dissolved solid)</td><td><strong>Two or more miscible liquids</strong> with different boiling points</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apparatus</td><td>Flask + condenser</td><td>Flask + <strong>fractionating column</strong> + condenser</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Pure water from salt water</td><td>Ethanol from water; petroleum fractions</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c21-pro-apparatus-for-measuring-ti","chem-prop",21,"Apparatus for measuring time, temperature, mass and volume","<p><strong>Time — stop-watch.</strong> <strong>Temperature — thermometer.</strong> <strong>Mass — balance.</strong> <strong>Volume of liquid — burette, volumetric pipette, measuring cylinder.</strong> <strong>Volume of gas — gas syringe</strong> (or measuring cylinder/burette upside-down over water). Choice: <strong>burette</strong> (to ±0.05 cm³) and <strong>volumetric pipette</strong> (fixed volume, very accurate) are more precise than a <strong>measuring cylinder</strong>; a gas syringe gives a direct reading but can stick or leak; collection over water doesn't work for very soluble gases.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-pro-interpreting-chromatograms","chem-prop",21,"Interpreting chromatograms","<p><strong>(a) Identify an unknown</strong> — it contains a known substance if a spot is at the <strong>same height (same Rf)</strong> as the known. <strong>(b) Purity</strong> — a <strong>pure</strong> substance gives <strong>one spot</strong>; an <strong>impure</strong> substance (mixture) gives <strong>two or more spots</strong>. A spot left on the baseline is insoluble in that solvent.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-pro-assessing-purity-with-melt","chem-prop",21,"Assessing purity with melting and boiling points","<p><strong>A pure substance has a sharp, fixed m.p. and b.p.</strong> that matches the data-book value. <strong>Impurities lower and widen the melting point</strong> (melts over a range) and <strong>raise the boiling point</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-pro-suggesting-a-separation-me","chem-prop",21,"Suggesting a separation method","<p>Insoluble solid + liquid → <strong>filtration</strong>. Soluble solid from solution → <strong>crystallisation</strong> (or evaporation). Liquid from a solution → <strong>simple distillation</strong>. Miscible liquids → <strong>fractional distillation</strong>. Coloured/soluble mixtures (inks, dyes, food colourings) → <strong>chromatography</strong>. Two solids → use a <strong>solvent</strong> that dissolves only one.</p>",0,""],["n-c21-fac-draw-the-chromatography-ba","chem-facts",21,"Chromatography baseline: drawn with what, and where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Draw the chromatography baseline in pencil and keep it above the solvent level.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Ink would separate; spots would dissolve into the solvent.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c21-fac-in-distillation-the-thermo","chem-facts",21,"Distillation: thermometer bulb position and condenser water flow?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In distillation, the thermometer bulb sits at the side-arm and water enters the condenser at the bottom.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Common diagram-labelling marks.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c22-rea-reactions-of-cations-with","chem-react",22,"Reactions of cations with aqueous sodium hydroxide","<p>Add a few drops, then <strong>excess</strong>.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Cation</th><th>Effect of aqueous sodium hydroxide</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aluminium, Al³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonium, NH₄⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Ammonia produced on warming</strong> (turns damp red litmus blue)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chromium(III), Cr³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., soluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light blue ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(II), Fe²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess, ppt. turns brown near surface on standing</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(III), Fe³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red-brown ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Zinc, Zn²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-rea-reactions-of-cations-with-2","chem-react",22,"Reactions of cations with aqueous ammonia","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Cation</th><th>Effect of aqueous ammonia</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aluminium, Al³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonium, NH₄⁺</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>No ppt. or very slight white ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chromium(III), Cr³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light blue ppt., soluble in excess, giving a dark blue solution</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(II), Fe²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess, ppt. turns brown near surface on standing</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(III), Fe³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red-brown ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Zinc, Zn²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-tests-for-anions","chem-test",22,"Tests for anions","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Anion</th><th>Test</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbonate, CO₃²⁻</strong></td><td>Add <strong>dilute acid</strong>, then test the gas with limewater</td><td><strong>Effervescence; carbon dioxide produced</strong> (limewater turns milky)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chloride, Cl⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with <strong>dilute nitric acid</strong>, then add <strong>aqueous silver nitrate</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Bromide, Br⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with dilute nitric acid, then add aqueous silver nitrate</td><td><strong>Cream ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iodide, I⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with dilute nitric acid, then add aqueous silver nitrate</td><td><strong>Yellow ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfate, SO₄²⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with <strong>dilute nitric acid</strong>, then add <strong>aqueous barium nitrate</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfite, SO₃²⁻</strong></td><td>Add a small volume of <strong>acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Nitrate, NO₃⁻</strong></td><td>Add <strong>aqueous sodium hydroxide</strong>, then <strong>aluminium foil</strong>; <strong>warm</strong> carefully</td><td><strong>Ammonia produced</strong> (damp red litmus → blue)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The acid is added first to <strong>remove carbonate ions</strong>, which would also give a precipitate. Use nitric acid (not hydrochloric) for the chloride test, otherwise you add chloride ions yourself.</p>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-identifying-cations","chem-test",22,"Identifying cations","<p>Use the two tables above: first add <strong>NaOH(aq)</strong> drop by drop then in excess; then a fresh sample with <strong>NH₃(aq)</strong>. <strong>Al³⁺ vs Zn²⁺</strong>: both white with NaOH and soluble in excess — <strong>zinc's ppt. dissolves in excess ammonia, aluminium's doesn't</strong>. <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>: white with NaOH but (almost) nothing with ammonia.</p>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-tests-for-gases","chem-test",22,"Tests for gases","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Gas</th><th>Test</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonia, NH₃</strong></td><td><strong>Damp red litmus paper</strong></td><td>Turns <strong>blue</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</strong></td><td>Bubble through <strong>limewater</strong></td><td>Turns <strong>milky</strong> (cloudy white)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chlorine, Cl₂</strong></td><td><strong>Damp litmus paper</strong></td><td><strong>Bleached</strong> (turns white; blue litmus goes red first)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hydrogen, H₂</strong></td><td><strong>Lighted splint</strong></td><td><strong>'Pops'</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Oxygen, O₂</strong></td><td><strong>Glowing splint</strong></td><td><strong>Relights</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfur dioxide, SO₂</strong></td><td><strong>Acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-flame-tests-for-metal-ions","chem-test",22,"Flame tests for metal ions","<p>Dip a clean nichrome/platinum wire (cleaned in concentrated HCl) into the solid, hold it in a <strong>roaring blue Bunsen flame</strong>.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Metal ion</th><th>Flame colour</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Lithium, Li⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sodium, Na⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Yellow</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Potassium, K⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Lilac</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Orange-red</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Barium, Ba²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light green</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Blue-green</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-fac-ppt-precipitate-always-say","chem-facts",22,"What must you always say about a ppt.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">\"ppt.\" = precipitate; always say the colour and what happens in excess.</p>",0,""]],"defs":[["d-c1-melting-point",1,"Melting point","<p>The temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid.</p>","<p>It has the same value as the freezing point. <strong>A pure substance has a sharp (fixed) melting point</strong>; impurities <strong>lower</strong> the melting point and make it melt over a <strong>range</strong> of temperatures.</p>",0],["d-c1-boiling-point",1,"Boiling point","<p>The temperature at which a liquid boils.</p>","<p>Bubbles of gas form <strong>throughout</strong> the liquid. <strong>A pure substance has a sharp (fixed) boiling point</strong>; impurities <strong>raise</strong> the boiling point. Pure water: m.p. <strong>0 °C</strong>, b.p. <strong>100 °C</strong> at atmospheric pressure.</p>",0],["d-c1-freezing-point",1,"Freezing point","<p>The temperature at which a liquid turns into a solid.</p>","<p>The <strong>same value as the melting point</strong> of that substance.</p>",0],["d-c1-evaporation",1,"Evaporation","<p>Change from liquid to gas that happens only at the surface of the liquid, at temperatures below the boiling point.</p>","<p>The fastest-moving particles at the surface have enough energy to escape. (Boiling happens throughout the liquid, only at the boiling point.)</p>",0],["d-c1-condensation",1,"Condensation","<p>Change from gas (vapour) to liquid.</p>","<p>When the gas is cooled; thermal energy is <strong>given out</strong> to the surroundings.</p>",0],["d-c1-volatility",1,"Volatility","<p>How easily a liquid evaporates (turns into a vapour).</p>","<p>A <strong>volatile</strong> liquid has a <strong>low boiling point</strong> because the forces between its particles are weak. Used to describe petroleum fractions.</p>",0],["d-c1-mixture",1,"Mixture","<p>Two or more substances mixed together but not chemically combined.</p>","<p>The substances keep their own properties and can be separated by <strong>physical methods</strong> (filtration, distillation, chromatography…). Composition can vary.</p>",0],["d-c1-solvent",1,"Solvent","<p>A substance that dissolves a solute.</p>","<p>(e.g. water, ethanol).</p>",0],["d-c1-solute",1,"Solute","<p>A substance that is dissolved in a solvent.</p>","",0],["d-c1-solution",1,"Solution","<p>A mixture of one or more solutes dissolved in a solvent.</p>","",0],["d-c1-diffusion",1,"Diffusion","<p>The net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration, as a result of their random movement.</p>","<p>Happens in <strong>gases and liquids</strong> (not solids, where particles only vibrate). Explained by <strong>kinetic particle theory</strong>: particles move randomly, collide and spread out until evenly mixed.</p>",0],["d-c1-saturated-solution",1,"Saturated solution","<p>A solution containing the maximum concentration of a solute dissolved in the solvent at a specified temperature.</p>","",0],["d-c1-residue",1,"Residue","<p>A substance that remains after evaporation, distillation, filtration or any similar process.</p>","",0],["d-c1-filtrate",1,"Filtrate","<p>A liquid or solution that has passed through a filter.</p>","",0],["d-c2-element",2,"Element","<p>A substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical methods.</p>","<p>All its atoms have the <strong>same number of protons</strong> (same proton number). e.g. iron, oxygen, carbon.</p>",0],["d-c2-compound",2,"Compound","<p>A substance made of two or more elements chemically combined in fixed proportions.</p>","<p>It has <strong>different properties</strong> from its elements and can only be separated by <strong>chemical reactions</strong>. e.g. water H₂O, sodium chloride NaCl.</p>",0],["d-c2-isotopes",2,"Isotopes","<p>Different atoms of the same element that have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p><p>Isotopes of the same element have the same chemical properties because they have the same number of electrons and therefore the same electronic configuration.</p>","<p>e.g. ¹²C and ¹⁴C; ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl.</p>",0],["d-c2-proton-number-atomic-number-z",2,"Proton number / atomic number (Z)","<p>The number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.</p>","<p>(It decides which element it is and its position in the Periodic Table.)</p>",0],["d-c2-mass-number-nucleon-number-a",2,"Mass number / nucleon number (A)","<p>The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.</p>","",0],["d-c2-relative-atomic-mass-ar",2,"Relative atomic mass, Ar","<p>The average mass of the isotopes of an element compared to 1/12th of the mass of an atom of ¹²C.</p>","<p>It is not a whole number for elements with several isotopes (e.g. Cl = 35.5).</p>",0],["d-c2-atom-and-its-structure",2,"Atom and its structure","<p>The atom has a central nucleus containing neutrons and protons, surrounded by electrons in shells.</p>","",0],["d-c2-electronic-configuration",2,"Electronic configuration","<p>The arrangement of electrons in shells.</p>","<p>Written as numbers, e.g. sodium <strong>2,8,1</strong>. Shells fill from the nucleus outwards: <strong>1st shell holds 2</strong>, <strong>2nd shell 8</strong>, <strong>3rd shell 8</strong> (for proton numbers 1–20; the 4th shell takes the next 2 electrons for K and Ca). Also for <strong>ions</strong>: Na⁺ 2,8 ; Cl⁻ 2,8,8 ; O²⁻ 2,8 ; Ca²⁺ 2,8,8.</p>",0],["d-c3-ionic-bond",3,"Ionic bond","<p>A strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.</p>","<p>Formed when a <strong>metal atom transfers electrons to a non-metal atom</strong>, so both get a full outer shell (noble gas configuration).</p>",0],["d-c3-covalent-bond",3,"Covalent bond","<p>Formed when a pair of electrons is shared between two atoms, leading to noble gas electronic configurations.</p>","<p>Usually between <strong>non-metal</strong> atoms. A <strong>double bond</strong> = two shared pairs (e.g. O=O, C=O, C=C); a <strong>triple bond</strong> = three shared pairs (N≡N).</p>",0],["d-c3-metallic-bonding",3,"Metallic bonding<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The electrostatic attraction between the positive ions in a giant metallic lattice and a 'sea' of delocalised electrons.</p>","",1],["d-c3-cation-and-anion",3,"Cation and anion","<p>Positive ions are called cations — formed when atoms (metals) lose electrons.</p><p>Negative ions are called anions — formed when atoms (non-metals) gain electrons.</p>","<p>e.g. Na → Na⁺ + e⁻. e.g. Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>",0],["d-c3-giant-ionic-lattice",3,"Giant ionic lattice<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A regular arrangement of alternating positive and negative ions.</p>","<p>Held together by strong electrostatic attraction in all directions (e.g. NaCl: each Na⁺ surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ and each Cl⁻ by 6 Na⁺).</p>",1],["d-c3-simple-molecular-substance",3,"Simple molecular substance","<p>Made of small molecules: strong covalent bonds within each molecule but only weak intermolecular forces between molecules.</p>","",0],["d-c3-giant-covalent-structure",3,"Giant covalent structure","<p>A huge network of atoms all joined by strong covalent bonds.</p>","<p>Diamond, graphite, silicon(IV) oxide (SiO₂). No separate molecules.</p>",0],["d-c4-molecular-formula",4,"Molecular formula","<p>The number and type of different atoms in one molecule.</p>","<p>e.g. ethane C₂H₆, glucose C₆H₁₂O₆.</p>",0],["d-c4-empirical-formula",4,"Empirical formula<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The simplest whole number ratio of the different atoms or ions in a compound.</p>","<p>e.g. ethane C₂H₆ → <strong>CH₃</strong>; glucose → <strong>CH₂O</strong>. For ionic compounds the formula <em>is</em> the empirical formula (NaCl, MgO).</p>",1],["d-c4-relative-molecular-mass-mr",4,"Relative molecular mass, Mr","<p>The sum of the relative atomic masses.</p>","<p>Add up the Ar of all the atoms in one molecule (or formula unit). e.g. H₂O = 2(1) + 16 = <strong>18</strong>. No units.</p>",0],["d-c4-relative-formula-mass-mr",4,"Relative formula mass, Mr","<p>The sum of the relative atomic masses.</p>","<p>Of all the atoms in the formula — the term <strong>used for ionic compounds</strong> (which have no molecules). e.g. CaCO₃ = 40 + 12 + 3(16) = <strong>100</strong>; Ca(OH)₂ = 40 + 2(16 + 1) = <strong>74</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c4-ionic-equation",4,"Ionic equation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Shows only the ions/particles that actually react.</p>","<p><strong>spectator ions</strong> (unchanged, present on both sides) are left out. e.g. neutralisation H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l); precipitation Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s); displacement Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s).</p>",1],["d-c5-avogadro-constant",5,"Avogadro constant<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The number of particles in one mole: 6.02 × 10²³.</p>","<p>The particles can be atoms, ions or molecules.</p>",1],["d-c5-mole-mol",5,"Mole (mol)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The unit of amount of substance; one mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles.</p>","<p>e.g. atoms, ions, molecules (the Avogadro constant). One mole of a substance has a mass equal to its <strong>Ar or Mr in grams</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c5-molar-mass",5,"Molar mass<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The mass of one mole of a substance, in g/mol.</p>","<p>Numerically equal to its Ar or Mr. e.g. molar mass of CO₂ = <strong>44 g/mol</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c5-limiting-reactant-limiting-rea",5,"Limiting reactant (limiting reagent)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The reactant that is completely used up first.</p>","<p>So it decides (limits) the amount of product formed; the other reactant is <strong>in excess</strong>. Find it by comparing the <strong>moles available</strong> with the <strong>mole ratio</strong> in the equation.</p>",1],["d-c5-percentage-yield",5,"Percentage yield<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The actual mass of product obtained as a percentage of the maximum (theoretical) mass that could be made.</p>","<p>See formula 7. Usually below 100% (incomplete reaction, losses during filtering/transferring, side reactions).</p>",1],["d-c5-percentage-purity",5,"Percentage purity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The mass of the pure substance in a sample as a percentage of the total mass of the (impure) sample.</p>","<p>See formula 8.</p>",1],["d-c5-percentage-composition-by-mass",5,"Percentage composition by mass<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The percentage of the mass of a compound that is due to one element.</p>","<p>% of element = (number of atoms × Ar ÷ Mr) × 100. e.g. % N in NH₄NO₃ = (2 × 14 ÷ 80) × 100 = <strong>35%</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c5-titration-calculations",5,"Titration (calculations)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Using measured volumes of two solutions that react exactly (one of known concentration) to find the moles, concentration or volume of the other.</p>","<p>(Method → Chapter 22, item 147.)</p>",1],["d-c5-concentration",5,"Concentration","<p>The amount of solute in a fixed volume of solution — measured in g/dm³ or mol/dm³.</p>","<p>1 dm³ = <strong>1000 cm³</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c5-molar-gas-volume",5,"Molar gas volume<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>One mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ (24 000 cm³) at room temperature and pressure (r.t.p.).</p>","",1],["d-c6-electrolysis",6,"Electrolysis","<p>The decomposition of an ionic compound, when molten or in aqueous solution, by the passage of an electric current.</p>","",0],["d-c6-electrolyte",6,"Electrolyte","<p>The molten or aqueous substance that undergoes electrolysis.</p>","<p>It conducts because its <strong>ions are free to move</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c6-electrolytic-cell-anode-and-ca",6,"Electrolytic cell — anode and cathode","<p>The anode is the positive electrode; the cathode is the negative electrode.</p>","<p>The apparatus for electrolysis: an electrolyte and two <strong>electrodes</strong> connected to a d.c. supply. <strong>Inert electrodes</strong> (graphite/carbon or platinum) do not react.</p>",0],["d-c6-spectator-ion",6,"Spectator ion<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An ion that is present in the reaction mixture but does not take part in the reaction.</p>","<p>It is the same on both sides, so it is left out of the ionic equation. e.g. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O.</p>",1],["d-c6-electroplating",6,"Electroplating","<p>Using electrolysis to coat a metal object with a thin layer of another metal.</p>","<p>Done <strong>to improve its appearance and its resistance to corrosion</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c6-hydrogen-oxygen-fuel-cell",6,"Hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell","<p>A cell that uses hydrogen and oxygen to produce electricity, with water as the only chemical product.</p>","<p>Overall: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.</p>",0],["d-c6-half-equation",6,"Half-equation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An ionic equation showing the electron transfer at one electrode.</p>","<p><strong>Cathode = reduction (gain of electrons)</strong>, e.g. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. <strong>Anode = oxidation (loss of electrons)</strong>, e.g. 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. (Remember: <strong>OIL RIG</strong> and <strong>\"Red Cat, An Ox\"</strong>.)</p>",1],["d-c7-endothermic-reaction",7,"Endothermic reaction","<p>A reaction that takes in thermal energy from the surroundings, leading to a decrease in the temperature of the surroundings.</p>","<p>e.g. thermal decomposition, photosynthesis, dissolving ammonium nitrate. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>ΔH is positive.</strong></p>",0],["d-c7-exothermic-reaction",7,"Exothermic reaction","<p>A reaction that transfers thermal energy to the surroundings, leading to an increase in the temperature of the surroundings.</p>","<p>e.g. combustion, neutralisation, respiration, metals with acids. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>ΔH is negative.</strong></p>",0],["d-c7-enthalpy-change-h",7,"Enthalpy change, ΔH<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The transfer of thermal energy during a reaction is called the enthalpy change, ΔH, of the reaction. ΔH is negative for exothermic reactions and positive for endothermic reactions.</p>","<p>Units: <strong>kJ/mol</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c7-bond-energy",7,"Bond energy<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The energy needed to break one mole of a particular covalent bond.</p>","<p>The same amount is released when that bond is made. Units kJ/mol.</p>",1],["d-c7-activation-energy-ea",7,"Activation energy, Ea<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The minimum energy that colliding particles must have to react.</p>","",1],["d-c7-bond-breaking-and-bond-making",7,"Bond breaking and bond making<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Bond breaking is an endothermic process; bond making is an exothermic process.</p>","<p>A reaction is <strong>exothermic</strong> if <strong>more energy is released making the new bonds</strong> in the products than is taken in breaking the bonds in the reactants; <strong>endothermic</strong> if more energy is taken in breaking bonds than released making them.</p>",1],["d-c8-collision-theory",8,"Collision theory<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Particles must collide with enough energy (at least the activation energy, Ea) to react.</p>","<p>The rate depends on: (a) the <strong>number of particles per unit volume</strong>, (b) the <strong>frequency of collisions</strong> between particles, (c) the <strong>kinetic energy</strong> of the particles, and (d) the <strong>activation energy</strong>. More <strong>successful collisions per second</strong> → faster rate.</p>",1],["d-c8-rate-of-reaction",8,"Rate of reaction","<p>How fast reactants are used up or products are formed — measured as change in amount (mass, volume of gas) per unit time, e.g. cm³/s.</p>","",0],["d-c8-catalyst",8,"Catalyst<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that increases the rate of a reaction and is unchanged at the end of the reaction.</p><p>A catalyst decreases the activation energy, Ea, of a reaction.</p>","<p>It provides a different reaction pathway with a lower Ea, so more collisions are successful.</p>",1],["d-c9-reversible-reaction",9,"Reversible reaction","<p>A reaction that can go in both directions (forwards and backwards), shown by the symbol ⇌.</p>","<p>Changing the conditions can change its direction.</p>",0],["d-c9-equilibrium",9,"Equilibrium<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reversible reaction in a closed system is at equilibrium when (a) the rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the reverse reaction and (b) the concentrations of reactants and products are no longer changing.</p>","<p>Both reactions are still happening (dynamic equilibrium). Concentrations are constant — <strong>not</strong> necessarily equal.</p>",1],["d-c9-contact-process",9,"Contact process<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The industrial process that makes sulfur trioxide (for sulfuric acid) from sulfur dioxide and oxygen.</p><p>2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g).</p><p>Sources: sulfur dioxide from burning sulfur or roasting sulfide ores; oxygen from air.</p><p>Conditions: 450 °C, 200 kPa (2 atm), vanadium(V) oxide (V₂O₅) catalyst.</p>","<p>The forward reaction is exothermic.</p>",1],["d-c9-haber-process",9,"Haber process<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The industrial manufacture of ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen.</p><p>N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g).</p><p>Sources: hydrogen from methane (natural gas); nitrogen from air.</p><p>Conditions: 450 °C, 20 000 kPa (200 atm), iron catalyst.</p>","<p>The forward reaction is exothermic. Ammonia is removed by cooling (it liquefies); unreacted N₂ and H₂ are recycled.</p>",1],["d-c10-redox-reaction",10,"Redox reaction","<p>A reaction involving simultaneous oxidation and reduction.</p>","<p>One substance is oxidised while another is reduced.</p>",0],["d-c10-oxidation-and-reduction-the-th",10,"Oxidation and reduction — the three definitions<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>W</p>","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Oxidation</th><th>Reduction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen [Core]</td><td><strong>Gain of oxygen</strong></td><td><strong>Loss of oxygen</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electrons <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Loss of electrons</strong></td><td><strong>Gain of electrons</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxidation number <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Increase in oxidation number</strong></td><td><strong>Decrease in oxidation number</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>OIL RIG</strong> — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons).</p>",1],["d-c10-oxidising-agent",10,"Oxidising agent<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that oxidises another substance and is itself reduced.</p>","<p>e.g. oxygen, chlorine, acidified potassium manganate(VII).</p>",1],["d-c10-reducing-agent",10,"Reducing agent<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that reduces another substance and is itself oxidised.</p>","<p>e.g. carbon, carbon monoxide, hydrogen, potassium iodide, reactive metals.</p>",1],["d-c11-acid",11,"Acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance whose aqueous solution contains H⁺ ions.</p><p>Acids are proton (H⁺) donors.</p>","<p>Acids have pH below 7 and turn litmus red. e.g. hydrochloric acid HCl, sulfuric acid H₂SO₄, nitric acid HNO₃, ethanoic acid CH₃COOH.</p>",1],["d-c11-neutralisation",11,"Neutralisation","<p>The reaction between an acid and a base (or alkali) to produce a salt and water.</p>","<p>Between an acid and an alkali the ionic equation is always: <strong>H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c11-amphoteric-oxide",11,"Amphoteric oxide<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An oxide that reacts with acids and with bases to produce a salt and water.</p>","<p><strong>Aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) and zinc oxide (ZnO)</strong> are amphoteric.</p>",1],["d-c11-acid-carbonate-how-co-is-made",11,"Acid + carbonate (how CO₂ is made in the lab)","<p>Acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide</p>","<p>e.g. CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g) (marble chips + dilute hydrochloric acid). <strong>Effervescence</strong>; the gas <strong>turns limewater milky</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c11-strong-acid",11,"Strong acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An acid that is completely dissociated (ionised) in aqueous solution.</p><p>e.g. hydrochloric acid: HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</p>","<p>Sulfuric and nitric acids are also strong acids.</p>",1],["d-c11-weak-acid",11,"Weak acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An acid that is partially dissociated in aqueous solution.</p><p>e.g. ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq).</p>","<p>Most molecules stay un-ionised, so the H⁺ concentration is lower.</p>",1],["d-c11-base-and-alkali",11,"Base and alkali<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Bases are oxides or hydroxides of metals; alkalis are soluble bases.</p><p>Bases are proton (H⁺) acceptors.</p>","<p>Aqueous alkalis contain <strong>OH⁻ ions</strong> (pH above 7; litmus blue). e.g. bases CuO, MgO; alkalis NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂, aqueous ammonia.</p>",1],["d-c11-acidic-oxide-and-basic-oxide",11,"Acidic oxide and basic oxide","<p>Acidic oxides are oxides of non-metals — e.g. SO₂, CO₂.</p><p>Basic oxides are oxides of metals — e.g. CuO, CaO.</p>","<p>Acidic oxides react with bases/alkalis and dissolve in water to give acidic solutions. Basic oxides react with acids. Related to <strong>metallic (basic) and non-metallic (acidic) character</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c12-salt",12,"Salt","<p>An ionic compound formed when the hydrogen of an acid is replaced by a metal (or ammonium) ion.</p>","<p>Made by reacting an acid with a metal, base, alkali or carbonate. Hydrochloric acid → <strong>chlorides</strong>; sulfuric acid → <strong>sulfates</strong>; nitric acid → <strong>nitrates</strong>; ethanoic acid → ethanoates.</p>",0],["d-c12-hydrated-and-anhydrous-substan",12,"Hydrated and anhydrous substances","<p>A hydrated substance is a substance that is chemically combined with water; an anhydrous substance is a substance containing no water.</p>","<p>e.g. hydrated copper(II) sulfate CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue) → anhydrous CuSO₄ (white) on heating.</p>",0],["d-c12-water-of-crystallisation",12,"Water of crystallisation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The water molecules present in hydrated crystals, including CuSO₄·5H₂O and CoCl₂·6H₂O.</p>","<p>(5 and 6 molecules of water per formula unit.)</p>",1],["d-c12-precipitation",12,"Precipitation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The formation of an insoluble solid (a precipitate) when two solutions are mixed.</p>","<p>Used to <strong>prepare insoluble salts</strong>: e.g. Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq); ionic equation <strong>Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c13-transition-elements",13,"Transition elements<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Metals in the central block of the Periodic Table (between Groups II and III) that (a) have high densities, (b) have high melting points, (c) form coloured compounds and (d) often act as catalysts, as elements and in compounds.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>They have ions with variable oxidation numbers, including iron(II) and iron(III).</strong> e.g. Fe, Cu, Ni, Cr, Mn, V.</p>",1],["d-c13-halide",13,"Halide","<p>A compound of a halogen with another element; halide ions are Cl⁻ (chloride), Br⁻ (bromide), I⁻ (iodide).</p>","<p>e.g. potassium iodide KI.</p>",0],["d-c13-halogen-displacement-reaction",13,"Halogen displacement reaction","<p>A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from an aqueous solution of its halide.</p>","<p>Reactivity: <strong>chlorine &gt; bromine &gt; iodine</strong>. e.g. <strong>Cl₂(aq) + 2KBr(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + Br₂(aq)</strong> (colourless → <strong>orange</strong>); <strong>Cl₂ + 2KI → 2KCl + I₂</strong> (→ <strong>brown</strong>); <strong>Br₂ + 2KI → 2KBr + I₂</strong> (→ brown). Iodine cannot displace chlorine or bromine. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Ionic: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ — a redox reaction (chlorine is reduced, bromide ions are oxidised). <strong>Explanation:</strong> the more reactive halogen is better at <strong>gaining an electron</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c13-noble-gases-inert-gases",13,"Noble gases (inert gases)","<p>Group VIII elements (He, Ne, Ar…) — unreactive, monatomic gases. They are unreactive because their atoms have a full outer shell of electrons.</p>","<p>So they do not need to lose, gain or share electrons (they exist as single atoms).</p>",0],["d-c13-oxidation-number-and-roman-num",13,"Oxidation number and Roman numerals<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The Roman numeral in a name shows the oxidation number (charge) of the element.</p>","<p>Transition metals have <strong>variable</strong> oxidation numbers: <strong>iron(II)</strong> Fe²⁺ (FeSO₄, green), <strong>iron(III)</strong> Fe³⁺ (Fe₂O₃, FeCl₃, orange-brown), copper(I) and copper(II). Rules for working them out: item 24, Chapter 10.</p>",1],["d-c13-period-and-group",13,"Period and group","<p>The Periodic Table is an arrangement of elements in periods and groups, in order of increasing proton number.</p>","<p><strong>Periods</strong> = horizontal <strong>rows</strong> (period number = <strong>number of occupied electron shells</strong>). <strong>Groups</strong> = vertical <strong>columns</strong> (Groups I–VII: group number = <strong>number of outer-shell electrons</strong>).</p>",0],["d-c14-alloy",14,"Alloy","<p>A mixture of a metal with other elements.</p><p>Brass is a mixture of copper and zinc.</p><p>Stainless steel is a mixture of iron and other elements such as chromium, nickel and carbon.</p><p>Alloys can be harder and stronger than the pure metals and are more useful.</p>","<p>The other elements are usually metals, sometimes carbon.</p>",0],["d-c15-displacement-reaction-metals",15,"Displacement reaction (metals)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from an aqueous solution of its ions (salt).</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)</strong> — blue colour fades, <strong>pink-brown copper</strong> forms, the mixture warms up. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Ionic: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. A less reactive metal <strong>cannot</strong> displace a more reactive one (Cu + MgSO₄ → no reaction).</p>",1],["d-c15-reactivity",15,"Reactivity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The relative reactivity of a metal depends on its tendency to form positive ions.</p>","<p>That is, its tendency to lose electrons: the more easily a metal forms positive ions, the more reactive it is.</p>",1],["d-c16-slag",16,"Slag","<p>Calcium silicate (CaSiO₃), formed in the blast furnace when calcium oxide (from limestone) reacts with the sandy impurity silicon(IV) oxide.</p>","<p>It is molten, <strong>less dense than iron</strong>, floats on top and is tapped off separately. Used for road-building.</p>",0],["d-c16-corrosion-and-rusting",16,"Corrosion and rusting","<p>Corrosion is the reaction of a metal with substances in its surroundings (oxygen, water…), which slowly destroys it.</p><p>Rusting is the corrosion of iron and steel.</p><p>Iron + water + oxygen → hydrated iron(III) oxide (rust)</p><p>Both water and oxygen are needed.</p>","<p>Rust is Fe₂O₃·xH₂O. Salt water speeds up rusting.</p>",0],["d-c16-galvanising",16,"Galvanising<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Coating iron or steel with a layer of zinc — an example of a barrier method and sacrificial protection.</p>","<p>It protects in two ways: as a <strong>barrier</strong> (keeps out water and oxygen) and by <strong>sacrificial protection</strong> (if scratched, the zinc corrodes instead of the iron).</p>",1],["d-c16-sacrificial-protection",16,"Sacrificial protection<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Attaching or coating iron/steel with a more reactive metal (zinc or magnesium), which corrodes instead of the iron.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Explanation: the more reactive metal <strong>loses electrons more easily</strong> (is oxidised in preference), so the <strong>iron does not lose electrons</strong> and is not oxidised. e.g. zinc/magnesium blocks on ships' hulls and pipelines.</p>",1],["d-c16-ore",16,"Ore","<p>A rock containing a metal compound from which the metal can be extracted economically.</p>","<p>Main ore of iron: <strong>hematite (iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃)</strong>. Main ore of aluminium: <strong>bauxite</strong> (aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃).</p>",0],["d-c17-acid-rain",17,"Acid rain","<p>Rain that is more acidic than normal because sulfur dioxide (SO₂) and oxides of nitrogen (NOₓ) dissolve in it.</p>","<p>This forms sulfuric and nitric acids. It damages buildings (limestone/marble), kills trees and fish in lakes, corrodes metals.</p>",0],["d-c17-flue-gas-desulfurisation",17,"Flue gas desulfurisation","<p>Removing sulfur dioxide from the waste (flue) gases of power stations by reacting it with calcium oxide (lime).</p>","<p>An acidic gas reacting with a basic oxide (CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃). Also: using <strong>low-sulfur fuels</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c17-photochemical-smog",17,"Photochemical smog","<p>A haze of polluting gases formed when oxides of nitrogen (from car engines) react in sunlight.</p>","<p>With other pollutants; causes <strong>respiratory problems</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c17-catalytic-converter",17,"Catalytic converter<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A device in a car exhaust that uses a catalyst (platinum/rhodium) to convert polluting gases into less harmful ones.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> e.g. <strong>2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂</strong> (removes both carbon monoxide and nitrogen monoxide).</p>",1],["d-c17-treatment-of-the-domestic-wate",17,"Treatment of the domestic water supply","<p>W</p>","<p>The syllabus lists three stages: (a) <strong>sedimentation and filtration to remove solids</strong> (screening first removes large objects); (b) <strong>use of carbon to remove tastes and odours</strong>; (c) <strong>chlorination to kill microbes</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c18-unsaturated-compound",18,"Unsaturated compound","<p>A compound with molecules in which one or more carbon–carbon bonds are not single bonds.</p>","<p>That is, it contains C=C. e.g. alkenes. An <strong>unsaturated hydrocarbon</strong> contains only C and H and has at least one C=C.</p>",0],["d-c18-homologous-series",18,"Homologous series<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A family of similar compounds with similar chemical properties due to the presence of the same functional group.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>General characteristics:</strong> (a) <strong>same functional group</strong>; (b) <strong>same general formula</strong>; (c) <strong>each member differs from the next by a –CH₂– unit</strong>; (d) <strong>a trend in physical properties</strong> (e.g. boiling point rises as the chain gets longer); (e) <strong>similar chemical properties</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c18-structural-isomers",18,"Structural isomers<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Compounds with the same molecular formula, but different structural formulae.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>C₄H₁₀: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ (butane) and CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃ (methylpropane)</strong>; <strong>C₄H₈: CH₃CH₂CH=CH₂ (but-1-ene) and CH₃CH=CHCH₃ (but-2-ene)</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c18-esters",18,"Esters<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Compounds made from a carboxylic acid and an alcohol.</p>","<p>Containing the <strong>–COO–</strong> group. Named <strong>alcohol part first (‑yl), acid part second (‑oate)</strong>: ethanol + ethanoic acid → <strong>ethyl ethanoate CH₃COOC₂H₅</strong>; methanol + propanoic acid → <strong>methyl propanoate</strong>; propan-1-ol + methanoic acid → <strong>propyl methanoate</strong>. Sweet, fruity smells.</p>",1],["d-c18-saturated-compound",18,"Saturated compound","<p>A compound with molecules in which all carbon–carbon bonds are single bonds.</p>","<p>e.g. alkanes.</p>",0],["d-c18-functional-group",18,"Functional group","<p>An atom or group of atoms that determine the chemical properties of a homologous series.</p>","<p>C=C (alkenes), –OH (alcohols), –COOH (carboxylic acids), –COO– (esters).</p>",0],["d-c18-hydrocarbon",18,"Hydrocarbon","<p>A compound that contains hydrogen and carbon only.</p>","<p>(Alkanes and alkenes.)</p>",0],["d-c18-displayed-formula",18,"Displayed formula","<p>Shows all the atoms and all the bonds in a molecule.</p>","<p>e.g. ethane: H–C–C–H with three H on each C drawn with lines.</p>",0],["d-c18-structural-formula",18,"Structural formula<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An unambiguous description of the way the atoms in a molecule are arranged.</p>","<p>Written without showing every bond, e.g. <strong>CH₂=CH₂, CH₃CH₂OH, CH₃COOCH₃</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c19-substitution-reaction",19,"Substitution reaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reaction in which one atom or group of atoms is replaced by another atom or group of atoms.</p>","<p>e.g. an H atom in an alkane replaced by Cl.</p>",1],["d-c19-photochemical-reaction",19,"Photochemical reaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reaction that needs light (ultraviolet) to provide the activation energy.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>methane + chlorine in UV light</strong>: <strong>CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl</strong> (chloromethane + hydrogen chloride) — monosubstitution. Ethane: C₂H₆ + Cl₂ → C₂H₅Cl + HCl.</p>",1],["d-c19-cracking-catalytic-cracking",19,"Cracking (catalytic cracking)","<p>The breaking down of larger alkane molecules into smaller alkanes, alkenes and hydrogen, using a high temperature and a catalyst.</p>","<p><strong>Reasons:</strong> (1) there is <strong>more demand for the smaller molecules</strong> (e.g. petrol) than the supply from fractional distillation, and less demand for the large ones; (2) it makes <strong>alkenes</strong>, used to make <strong>polymers</strong> and other chemicals (e.g. ethanol); (3) it makes <strong>hydrogen</strong> (e.g. for the Haber process).</p>",0],["d-c19-fractional-distillation-of-pet",19,"Fractional distillation of petroleum","<p>Separating petroleum (a mixture of hydrocarbons) into fractions with different boiling-point ranges.</p>","<p>Petroleum is vaporised and passed into a <strong>fractionating column</strong> which is <strong>hot at the bottom and cooler at the top</strong>; each fraction <strong>condenses</strong> at the height where the temperature is just below its boiling point. (Fractions → Chapter 20.)</p>",0],["d-c19-addition-of-bromine-the-test-f",19,"Addition of bromine — the test for unsaturation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Aqueous bromine (bromine water) turns from orange (brown) to colourless with an alkene (unsaturated); it stays orange with an alkane (saturated).</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Addition reaction: <strong>CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br</strong> (1,2-dibromoethane).</p>",1],["d-c19-hydrogenation",19,"Hydrogenation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Addition of hydrogen to an alkene in the presence of a nickel catalyst.</p>","<p>To form an alkane: <strong>CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃CH₃</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c19-hydration-catalytic-addition-o",19,"Hydration (catalytic addition of steam)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Addition of steam to an alkene in the presence of an acid catalyst.</p>","<p>To form an alcohol: <strong>CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O → CH₃CH₂OH</strong>. Industrial conditions for ethanol: <strong>300 °C, 6000 kPa (60 atm), acid catalyst</strong> (phosphoric acid).</p>",1],["d-c19-fermentation",19,"Fermentation","<p>Making ethanol from aqueous glucose using yeast, at 25–35 °C, in the absence of oxygen.</p>","<p><strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂</strong>. The yeast's enzymes are the catalyst. Ethanol is separated by fractional distillation.</p>",0],["d-c19-esterification",19,"Esterification<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The reaction of a carboxylic acid with an alcohol, using an acid catalyst (concentrated sulfuric acid), to form an ester and water.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH → CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O</strong> (ethyl ethanoate).</p>",1],["d-c19-bonding-in-alkanes-and-alkenes",19,"Bonding in alkanes and alkenes","<p>W</p>","<p><strong>The bonding in alkanes is single covalent and alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons.</strong> <strong>The bonding in alkenes includes a double carbon–carbon covalent bond and alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons.</strong></p>",0],["d-c19-addition-reaction",19,"Addition reaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reaction in which only one product is formed.</p>","<p>The reagent adds across the C=C double bond of an alkene (bromine, hydrogen, steam; also polymerisation).</p>",1],["d-c20-polymers",20,"Polymers","<p>Large molecules built up from many smaller molecules called monomers.</p>","",0],["d-c20-monomer",20,"Monomer","<p>One of the small molecules that join together to make a polymer.</p>","<p>e.g. ethene is the monomer of poly(ethene).</p>",0],["d-c20-polymerisation-addition-and-co",20,"Polymerisation — addition and condensation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Joining many monomers to make a polymer.</p><p>Addition polymerisation: monomers with a C=C double bond join and the polymer is the only product.</p><p>Condensation polymerisation: monomers with two functional groups join and a small molecule (water) is eliminated at each link.</p>","<p>Addition example: <strong>ethene → poly(ethene)</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Condensation polymerisation is Extended only; examples are nylon and PET.</p>",1],["d-c20-polyamide-nylon",20,"Polyamide — nylon<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A condensation polymer made from a dicarboxylic acid and a diamine; the monomers are joined by amide links (–CO–NH–).</p>","<p>Nylon is a polyamide. Water is eliminated at each link.</p>",1],["d-c20-polyester-pet",20,"Polyester — PET<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A condensation polymer made from a dicarboxylic acid and a diol; the monomers are joined by ester links (–CO–O–).</p><p>PET can be converted back into monomers and re-polymerised.</p>","<p>PET is a polyester, so it can be recycled this way. (The full name of PET is not required.)</p>",1],["d-c20-fossil-fuels",20,"Fossil fuels","<p>W</p>","<p><strong>Coal, natural gas and petroleum.</strong> <strong>Methane</strong> is the main constituent of <strong>natural gas</strong>. <strong>Petroleum is a mixture of hydrocarbons.</strong></p>",0],["d-c20-plastics",20,"Plastics","<p>Plastics are made from polymers.</p>","<p>Most are <strong>non-biodegradable</strong> (not broken down by microorganisms).</p>",0],["d-c21-control-variable",21,"Control variable","<p>A variable that is kept the same during an investigation.</p>","<p>So that only the independent variable affects the result (a <strong>fair test</strong>). e.g. volume and concentration of acid, temperature, mass of solid.</p>",0],["d-c21-independent-variable",21,"Independent variable","<p>The variable that is changed by the scientist in an experiment.</p>","<p>Plotted on the <strong>x-axis</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c21-dependent-variable",21,"Dependent variable","<p>The variable that is observed or measured.</p>","<p>It may change when the independent variable is changed. Plotted on the <strong>y-axis</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c21-paper-chromatography",21,"Paper chromatography<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A method for separating mixtures of soluble substances (e.g. dyes) using a suitable solvent moving up absorbent paper.</p>","<p>Draw a <strong>pencil</strong> baseline (ink would run); put spots of the mixture and known substances on it; stand the paper in solvent <strong>below the baseline</strong>; the solvent moves up and carries each substance a different distance (depending on its solubility and attraction to the paper). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Colourless substances</strong> are made visible by spraying with a <strong>locating agent</strong> (specific agents not required).</p>",1],["d-c21-rf-value",21,"Rf value<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Rf = distance travelled by the substance ÷ distance travelled by the solvent.</p>","<p>Both distances are measured from the baseline. Always <strong>less than 1</strong>; no units. The same substance has the same Rf in the same solvent → used to identify substances.</p>",1],["d-c22-acid-base-titration",22,"Acid–base titration","<p>A method of finding exactly how much acid reacts with a measured volume of alkali (or vice versa).</p>","<p>Use a <strong>volumetric pipette</strong> to put a fixed volume (e.g. 25.0 cm³) of alkali into a conical flask; add a few drops of a <strong>suitable indicator</strong> (e.g. methyl orange or thymolphthalein); add acid from a <strong>burette</strong>, swirling, <strong>until the indicator just changes colour</strong> — the <strong>end-point</strong>. Record the burette readings; repeat until <strong>concordant</strong> results (within 0.10 cm³) and take the mean. Calculation → Chapter 5.</p>",0],["d-c22-titre",22,"Titre","<p>The volume of solution added from the burette to reach the end-point.</p>","<p>Titre = final reading − initial reading.</p>",0],["d-c22-end-point",22,"End-point","<p>The point at which the indicator just changes colour, showing the reaction is complete.</p>","<p>Methyl orange: <strong>yellow → orange/red</strong> when acid is added to alkali; thymolphthalein: <strong>blue → colourless</strong>.</p>",0]]});
